citeturn488299academia50\",7829,7856,[617,618],[],\"([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.17734?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[1182],[],{\"_46\":1183,\"_34\":618,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":1184,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":1185,\"_1130\":1186,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},\"Harnessing LLM Agents with Skill Programs\",1779068111,[],[1187],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":1189},\"academia\",50,\"citeturn126048search1\",6001,6025,[635,636],[],\"([W3C](https://www.w3.org/TR/scxml/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[1198],[],{\"_46\":1067,\"_34\":636,\"_664\":1145,\"_663\":1094,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":1199,\"_1130\":1200,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[1201],{\"_665\":471,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":471},\"citeturn834369search3turn834369search2\",4373,4415,[629,630,631,632],[],\"([Temporal](https://go.temporal.io/platform-hub/ai-engineering/ai-reference-architecture?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[1210],[],{\"_46\":1146,\"_34\":630,\"_664\":1147,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":1211,\"_1130\":1212,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[1215],[1213,1214],{\"_665\":844,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":844},{\"_665\":844,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":1047},{\"_46\":851,\"_34\":632,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_664\":1147},\"citeturn834369search1turn834369search0\",4072,4114,[621,622,627,628],[],\"([GitHub](https://github.com/temporalio/temporal/blob/main/docs/architecture/README.md?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[1224],[],{\"_46\":1225,\"_34\":628,\"_664\":1151,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":1226,\"_1130\":1227,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},\"temporal/docs/architecture/README.md at main · temporalio/temporal · GitHub\",[1230],[1228,1229],{\"_665\":844,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":471},{\"_665\":844,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":666},{\"_46\":859,\"_34\":622,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_664\":1231},\"Temporal Community Forum\",\"citeturn488299academia51\",1347,1374,[619,620],[],\"([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.10319?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[1240],[],{\"_46\":1241,\"_34\":620,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":1242,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":1243,\"_1130\":1244,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},\"Do Personalized Skills Help Coding Agents? An Empirical Study of Developer Interaction Histories\",1786405262,[],[1245],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":1246},51,\"citeturn488299academia48\",1227,[615,616],[],\"([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.15401?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[1254],[],{\"_46\":1148,\"_34\":616,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":1255,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":1256,\"_1130\":1257,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},1773674191,[],[1258],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":1259},48,\"citeturn488299search1turn488299search2\",750,792,[625,626,633,634],[],\"([GitHub](https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent/blob/main/website/docs/user-guide/features/skills.md?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[1268],[],{\"_46\":1150,\"_34\":626,\"_664\":1151,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":1269,\"_1130\":1270,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[1273],[1271,1272],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":471},{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":1047},{\"_46\":1033,\"_34\":634,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_664\":1274},\"Hermes Agent\",\"stop\",\"stop_tokens\",[1278],200002,\"content_type\",\"text\",\"parts\",[1283],\"Yes. I think the smart solution is to **stop treating “repeatable engineering methodology” as a skill**.\\n\\nA skill is useful for something like:\\n\\n\u003e “Here are good practices for reviewing a PostgreSQL migration.”\\n\\nIt is a bad representation for:\\n\\n\u003e “Run requirements elicitation, freeze acceptance criteria, create hidden tests, implement, mutation-test, fuzz, canary, obtain human approval, record provenance, and only then close the phase.”\\n\\nThose are different kinds of things.\\n\\nHermes's own documentation makes the limitation fairly clear: a Hermes skill is fundamentally an on-demand knowledge document—`SKILL.md` contains procedure, pitfalls, verification advice, and the agent itself can create or modify these skills after learning a workflow. citeturn488299search1turn488299search2 That is excellent for **procedural memory**. It is not a strong execution contract.\\n\\nAnd recent evidence is pretty brutal about overusing this pattern. SWE-Skills-Bench evaluated 49 public software-engineering skills: 39 produced **zero pass-rate improvement**, three actually degraded performance, and only seven showed meaningful improvements. Version-mismatched guidance was one observed source of harm. citeturn488299academia48 Another 2026 study of personalized coding-agent skills found only small and inconsistent gains compared with no skill. citeturn488299academia51\\n\\nSo your experience is consistent with emerging evidence.\\n\\n## I would separate six concepts\\n\\n```text\\n                    METHODOLOGY\\n                         │\\n            compiled into executable flow\\n                         │\\n                         ▼\\n                  WORKFLOW / STATE\\n                         │\\n          ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐\\n          │              │              │\\n       POLICIES       ACTIVITIES     EVALUATORS\\n      invariants       tools/LLMs      tests\\n        gates          side effects    oracles\\n          │              │              │\\n          └──────────────┼──────────────┘\\n                         ▼\\n                    RECEIPTS\\n                         │\\n                         ▼\\n                      FOSSIL\\n\\nSkills sit over here:\\n\\n   SKILL / PLAYBOOK\\n   advisory knowledge\\n   examples\\n   heuristics\\n   references\\n```\\n\\nThe important separation is:\\n\\n**Skills tell an agent how.**\\n\\n**Workflows determine what state comes next.**\\n\\n**Policies say what is forbidden or required.**\\n\\n**Activities actually do things.**\\n\\n**Evaluators decide whether externally observable conditions were satisfied.**\\n\\n**FOSSIL remembers why, what happened, and what evidence exists.**\\n\\nThat architecture is considerably harder for an agent to casually bypass.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# The methodology should be executable\\n\\nSuppose we define an engineering methodology:\\n\\n```yaml\\nmethodology: bounded-feature-development\\nversion: 3\\n\\nstates:\\n\\n  intent:\\n    activity: intent.compile\\n    exit_when:\\n      - no_critical_ambiguity\\n      - acceptance_defined\\n\\n  spec_attack:\\n    activities:\\n      - critic.requirements\\n      - invariant.discover\\n\\n    exit_when:\\n      - contradictions_resolved\\n\\n  implement:\\n    activity: coder.implement\\n\\n  verify:\\n    parallel:\\n      - tests.contract\\n      - tests.hidden\\n      - mutation\\n      - fuzz\\n      - synthetic_users\\n\\n    exit_when:\\n      - required_checks_pass\\n\\n  human_acceptance:\\n    when:\\n      - human_judgment_required\\n\\n  closeout:\\n    activity: evidence.closeout\\n```\\n\\nThis isn't a prompt saying:\\n\\n\u003e “Remember to clarify requirements before coding.”\\n\\nThe runtime physically cannot move:\\n\\n```text\\nINTENT → IMPLEMENT\\n```\\n\\nunless the declared transition conditions permit it.\\n\\nThat is a qualitatively different system.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Existing workflow systems give us a mature pattern\\n\\nTemporal is probably the closest conceptual precedent.\\n\\nTemporal separates **deterministic workflow logic** from **Activities that perform side effects**. Workflow state is reconstructed by replaying an append-only event history; the workflow must produce the same decisions for the same history. Side-effecting operations belong outside that deterministic logic. citeturn834369search1turn834369search0\\n\\nTheir recent AI reference architecture applies that rule directly to agents: LLM calls, tool calls, API calls and database reads are treated as Activities rather than deterministic workflow logic, with their observed results recorded into workflow history. citeturn834369search3turn834369search2\\n\\nThat is **extremely applicable** to what you're describing.\\n\\nImagine:\\n\\n```text\\nDETERMINISTIC CONTROL\\n─────────────────────\\n\\nstate = INTENT\\n\\nif intent_report.critical_unknowns \u003e 0:\\n    state = HUMAN_CLARIFICATION\\nelse:\\n    state = SPEC_ATTACK\\n```\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n```text\\nNONDETERMINISTIC ACTIVITY\\n─────────────────────────\\n\\nresult = call_model(\\n    model=\\\"whatever-frontier-model\\\",\\n    prompt=...\\n)\\n```\\n\\nThe model result gets recorded.\\n\\nOn replay, we don't ask the model to reinvent the past.\\n\\nWe replay:\\n\\n```text\\nactivity_result_9182\\n```\\n\\nThat gives you both repeatability and the ability to swap models.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# This solves one of the biggest agent problems\\n\\nToday the methodology often lives inside the model's context:\\n\\n```text\\nsystem prompt\\nAGENTS.md\\nSKILL.md\\nconversation history\\n```\\n\\nSo methodology execution depends on:\\n\\n\u003e Did the model remember instruction #37 at the right moment?\\n\\nThat's inherently soft.\\n\\nInstead:\\n\\n```text\\nRuntime:\\n\\\"You are currently in VERIFY_MUTATION.\\\"\\n\\nAgent:\\n\\\"I think we're finished.\\\"\\n\\nRuntime:\\n\\\"No. Mutation threshold not satisfied.\\\"\\n\\nAgent:\\n\\\"Let's proceed anyway.\\\"\\n\\nRuntime:\\n\\\"Transition unavailable.\\\"\\n```\\n\\nNow the agent is **inside the process**.\\n\\nIt isn't responsible for remembering the process.\\n\\nThat is the architectural change.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# State machines are a mature abstraction for exactly this\\n\\nYou don't need to invent the fundamental model.\\n\\nThe W3C standardized SCXML as a general-purpose event-based state-machine model with explicit states, transitions, conditions, entry/exit behavior, parallel states and defined deterministic execution semantics. citeturn126048search1\\n\\nI'm not saying “use XML.”\\n\\nI probably wouldn't.\\n\\nI'm saying borrow the semantics:\\n\\n```text\\nstate\\nevent\\ntransition\\nguard\\naction\\nparallel branch\\nterminal state\\n```\\n\\nFor agent engineering that might become:\\n\\n```text\\nState:\\n  REQUIREMENTS\\n\\nEvent:\\n  clarification_received\\n\\nGuard:\\n  critical_ambiguity_count == 0\\n\\nTransition:\\n  REQUIREMENTS → SPECIFICATION\\n\\nActions:\\n  freeze_requirement_snapshot\\n  generate_holdouts\\n```\\n\\nThat's far more robust than:\\n\\n```text\\nskill step 7:\\n\\\"Once you've clarified enough, start planning.\\\"\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Where skills remain useful\\n\\nI wouldn't delete skills.\\n\\nI'd **demote them**.\\n\\nA smart hierarchy might be:\\n\\n```text\\nPolicy\\n    non-negotiable constraint\\n\\nWorkflow\\n    deterministic process/control flow\\n\\nActivity\\n    executable implementation\\n\\nEvaluator\\n    machine/human oracle\\n\\nSkill\\n    optional procedural guidance\\n\\nReference\\n    passive knowledge\\n```\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n```text\\nPOLICY\\n\\\"Never promote a closeout directly as evidence.\\\"\\n\\nWORKFLOW\\n\\\"requirements → implementation → verification → promotion\\\"\\n\\nACTIVITY\\n\\\"run mutation tests\\\"\\n\\nEVALUATOR\\n\\\"mutation score \u003e= configured threshold\\\"\\n\\nSKILL\\n\\\"Techniques for designing high-value mutation operators.\\\"\\n\\nREFERENCE\\n\\\"Mutation-testing research papers.\\\"\\n```\\n\\nNow a skill failing to activate doesn't invalidate your methodology.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# There is actually emerging research supporting this direction too\\n\\nHASP, published this year, explicitly criticizes ordinary textual agent skills because they are **advisory** and lack mechanisms controlling when/how they intervene. Their proposed “Skill Programs” turn learned procedures into executable program functions that activate on particular failure-prone states and intervene in the agent loop. They report substantial gains over text-like skill approaches in several evaluated settings. citeturn488299academia50\\n\\nThat's very close to the architectural distinction we're making:\\n\\n\u003e move important methodology out of prose and into executable control.\\n\\nI would go even further than HASP for engineering, though.\\n\\nNot:\\n\\n```text\\nLLM loop + executable skill interventions\\n```\\n\\nbut:\\n\\n```text\\ndeterministic engineering workflow\\n        ↓\\nLLMs invoked as replaceable workers\\n```\\n\\nThe workflow is the boss.\\n\\nThe model is a worker.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Repeatability also needs hermetic execution\\n\\nIf the methodology says:\\n\\n```text\\nrun test suite\\n```\\n\\nbut one agent runs Python 3.11 with dependency X and another runs Python 3.13 with dependency Y, you've only made the **control flow** reproducible.\\n\\nNot the work.\\n\\nBazel's hermeticity principles are useful here: reproducibility comes from isolating builds from host-machine state and pinning tool/dependency identity so equal inputs produce equal outputs. citeturn488299search0\\n\\nFor the AI engineering system that means recording or pinning where material:\\n\\n```text\\nsource commit\\ntool version\\nruntime/container\\ndependency lock\\nworkflow version\\npolicy version\\nmodel identity\\nprompt/activity version\\ninput artifacts\\nenvironment profile\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```text\\nRUN-3928\\n```\\n\\ncan actually be meaningfully replayed or investigated.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# But don't make every process deterministic\\n\\nThis is an important correction.\\n\\nResearch isn't deterministic.\\n\\nRequirements interviews aren't deterministic.\\n\\nCritique isn't deterministic.\\n\\nFuzz case generation isn't deterministic.\\n\\nLLM behavior certainly isn't deterministic.\\n\\nThe **orchestration should be deterministic; the activities do not need to be.**\\n\\nThat's the Temporal lesson.\\n\\n```text\\nWorkflow says:\\n\\n1. invoke requirement critic\\n2. persist result\\n3. if critical ambiguity → human\\n4. otherwise proceed\\n```\\n\\nThe critic itself might return something completely different if rerun.\\n\\nThat's okay.\\n\\nFor this execution, its result was:\\n\\n```text\\nartifact sha256:...\\nactivity version:...\\nmodel:...\\n```\\n\\nand that becomes part of history.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Methodologies should themselves be versioned and testable\\n\\nThis gets interesting.\\n\\nImagine:\\n\\n```text\\nengineering-methodology-v1\\n```\\n\\nrequires:\\n\\n```text\\nintent\\nimplement\\ntests\\ncloseout\\n```\\n\\nThen after real failures we introduce:\\n\\n```text\\nengineering-methodology-v2\\n```\\n\\nadding:\\n\\n```text\\nspec critique\\nhidden tests\\nmutation testing\\n```\\n\\nDon't silently rewrite workflows already in progress.\\n\\nNew work uses v2.\\n\\nExisting runs preserve v1 provenance.\\n\\nThen you can **benchmark the methodology itself**:\\n\\n```text\\n100 historical tasks\\n\\nv1:\\n  downstream human corrections = 21\\n  escaped bugs = 14\\n  human minutes = 340\\n\\nv2:\\n  downstream corrections = 9\\n  escaped bugs = 6\\n  human minutes = 225\\n```\\n\\nNow even the engineering process is being improved empirically.\\n\\nThat's much more useful than:\\n\\n\u003e “We updated our coding skill because it seemed better.”\\n\\n---\\n\\n# We can also fuzz methodologies\\n\\nThis is where your free-inference idea gets powerful.\\n\\nThe methodology itself becomes an object under test.\\n\\nAgents can generate:\\n\\n```text\\nWhat happens if human never responds?\\n\\nWhat happens if hidden test infrastructure fails?\\n\\nWhat happens if verifier and implementer disagree?\\n\\nWhat happens if a model call times out?\\n\\nWhat if requirement state changes during implementation?\\n\\nWhat if migration fails halfway?\\n\\nWhat if two verification branches contradict each other?\\n\\nWhat if user cancels the requirement?\\n\\nWhat if a previous decision becomes superseded?\\n```\\n\\nThen execute those transitions against the workflow engine.\\n\\nState-machine/model-checking tools can test impossible or unsafe transitions.\\n\\nNow we're not merely testing **software**.\\n\\nWe're testing the **software-development process**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Methodologies should compose, but very carefully\\n\\nThis is where I would avoid repeating the skills mistake.\\n\\nDon't create:\\n\\n```text\\n1,000 methodologies\\n```\\n\\nInstead have a small base process:\\n\\n```text\\nINTENT\\n  ↓\\nSPECIFY\\n  ↓\\nIMPLEMENT\\n  ↓\\nVERIFY\\n  ↓\\nACCEPT\\n  ↓\\nCLOSE\\n```\\n\\nThen risk profiles activate additional **branches or gates**:\\n\\n```text\\nlow risk\\n    basic tests\\n\\ndata migration\\n    + backup proof\\n    + rollback\\n    + dual-read check\\n\\nconcurrency\\n    + formal/state-model check\\n    + stress\\n\\nuser-facing AI\\n    + synthetic conversation fuzz\\n    + human acceptance\\n\\ndurable schema\\n    + compatibility\\n    + migration\\n    + historical replay\\n```\\n\\nSo instead of skills:\\n\\n```text\\nskill_for_database\\nskill_for_frontend\\nskill_for_rag\\nskill_for_migration\\nskill_for_chatbot\\n```\\n\\nyou get:\\n\\n```text\\nBASE ENGINEERING WORKFLOW\\n\\n+ risk modules\\n+ domain evaluators\\n+ optional procedural skills\\n```\\n\\nMuch smaller combinatorial surface.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# And FOSSIL fits naturally without owning execution\\n\\nI would **not** make FOSSIL the workflow engine.\\n\\nThat's scope explosion again.\\n\\nInstead:\\n\\n```text\\nWorkflow runtime\\n       │\\n       ├── emits durable receipts\\n       ├── produces decisions\\n       ├── produces observations\\n       ├── references artifacts\\n       └── records methodology/activity versions\\n                │\\n                ▼\\n             FOSSIL\\n```\\n\\nFOSSIL can answer:\\n\\n```text\\nWhich methodology was used?\\n\\nWhich version?\\n\\nWhich states were traversed?\\n\\nWhich gates failed?\\n\\nWhich agent executed each activity?\\n\\nWhat evidence opened the next gate?\\n\\nWhich human decision resolved ambiguity?\\n\\nWhich workflow version produced fewer escaped failures?\\n\\nWhy was methodology v3 adopted?\\n```\\n\\nBut FOSSIL does not decide the next runtime state.\\n\\nThat keeps the knowledge plane clean.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## So I'd replace “skills everywhere” with this\\n\\n**A small deterministic methodology runtime + versioned workflow definitions + typed activities + policies + evaluators + durable receipts.**\\n\\nSkills survive only for things that are inherently fuzzy procedural expertise.\\n\\nThe dividing rule I'd use is:\\n\\n\u003e **If failing to follow an instruction can make the engineering process invalid, it should not exist only as prompt text.**\\n\\nMake it a guard, state transition, type check, permission boundary, test, executable policy, or runtime constraint.\\n\\nIf it's:\\n\\n\u003e “Here are useful tactics and examples that may help you perform this task better,”\\n\\nthen a skill is exactly the right mechanism.\\n\\nThat distinction, more than any particular framework, is probably what prevents an “AI work OS” from degenerating into 500 markdown skills that agents sometimes remember to read.\",\"role\",\"assistant\",{\"_1287\":1288},\"real_author\",\"tool:web\",{\"_454\":458,\"_461\":1292,\"_47\":1293,\"_465\":1294,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":1295,\"_474\":475},\"5fa2f14e-c620-4dc2-90df-f2ac46345ddb\",[74],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":1307},1786907748.691047,{\"_1279\":1305,\"_465\":1306},{\"_1296\":1297,\"_1298\":739,\"_1299\":1300,\"_1301\":1302,\"_1303\":1304,\"_493\":494,\"_495\":496,\"_497\":498,\"_499\":500,\"_501\":500,\"_490\":60,\"_502\":60,\"_59\":60,\"_503\":504,\"_505\":14,\"_506\":1290,\"_507\":508,\"_509\":26,\"_510\":511,\"_512\":10},\"reasoning_status\",\"reasoning_ended\",\"finished_duration_sec\",\"reasoning_start_time\",1786907724.191401,\"reasoning_end_time\",1786907748.6728256,\"reasoning_recap_type\",\"collapse\",\"reasoning_recap\",\"Worked for 24s\",{},{\"_454\":1290,\"_461\":1311,\"_47\":1312,\"_465\":1313,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":1314,\"_474\":475},\"24e2aab2-7d7f-4bcf-9047-d3c129410bd8\",[458],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":1386},1786907748.686788,{\"_1279\":1376,\"_1376\":137