equirements Elicitation with an Experience Ontology\",1778140983,[],[1977],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":1047},\"citeturn104105academia0\",8378,8404,[1592,1593],[],\"([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.26233?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[1986],[],{\"_46\":1896,\"_34\":1593,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":1987,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":1988,\"_1130\":1989,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},1774605386,[],[1990],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":666},\"citeturn899256academia2\",7759,7785,[1596,1597],[],\"([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.21384?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[1999],[],{\"_46\":2000,\"_34\":1597,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":2001,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2002,\"_1130\":2003,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},\"SpecBench: Measuring Reward Hacking in Long-Horizon Coding Agents\",1779295311,[],[2004],{\"_665\":471,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":1047},\"citeturn899256academia1\",6991,7017,[1598,1599],[],\"([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.30314?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[2013],[],{\"_46\":2014,\"_34\":1599,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":2015,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2016,\"_1130\":2017,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},\"SpecBench: Evaluating Specification-Level Reasoning for Software Engineering LLM Agents\",1779990841,[],[2018],{\"_665\":471,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":471},\"citeturn104105academia1\",6427,6453,[1600,1601],[],\"([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.00711?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[2027],[],{\"_46\":2028,\"_34\":1601,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":2029,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2030,\"_1130\":2031,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},\"ClarifyCodeBench: Evaluating LLMs on Clarifying Ambiguous Requirements for Code Generation\",1782899939,[],[2032],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":471},4551,4577,[1590,1591],[],[2039],[],{\"_46\":1959,\"_34\":1591,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":1960,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2040,\"_1130\":2041,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[2042],{\"_665\":471,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":666},3262,3288,[1594,1595],[],[2049],[],{\"_46\":1973,\"_34\":1595,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":1974,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2050,\"_1130\":2051,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[2052],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":1047},\"citeturn104105academia3\",982,1008,[1604,1605],[],\"([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.09072?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[2061],[],{\"_46\":2062,\"_34\":1605,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":2063,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2064,\"_1130\":2065,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},\"A Unified Issue Resolution Benchmark for Requirement Clarification, Planning, and Code Generation for Coding Agents\",1786332124,[],[2066],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":844},689,715,[1600,1601],[],[2073],[],{\"_46\":2028,\"_34\":1601,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":2029,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2074,\"_1130\":2075,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[2076],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":471},491,517,[1592,1593],[],[2083],[],{\"_46\":1896,\"_34\":1593,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":1987,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2084,\"_1130\":2085,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[2086],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":666},[1278],[2089],\"Yes. The best solution I can see is **not “make Codex ask more questions.”** It is a separate **Intent/Requirements Broker** that sits *before* implementation and owns the transition from vague human request → executable behavioral contract.\\n\\nRecent evidence makes that separation look justified. In *Ask or Assume?*, an uncertainty-aware multi-agent scaffold explicitly separated underspecification detection from code execution and improved SWE-bench-style resolution from 61.2% to 69.4%. citeturn104105academia0 ClarifyCodeBench independently finds that strong coding ability does **not** imply strong clarification ability, and performance falls as multiple ambiguities accumulate. citeturn104105academia1 Most recently, SWE-RPG evaluated Claude Code, Codex and OpenCode across clarification → planning → implementation and found implicit-requirement recovery was the largest diagnosed failure source, accounting for roughly 24.5–46.0% of runs depending on configuration. citeturn104105academia3\\n\\nSo I would build it as its own subsystem.\\n\\n## The architecture I would use\\n\\n```text\\nHuman request\\n      │\\n      ▼\\n┌─────────────────────────────┐\\n│      INTENT BROKER          │\\n│                             │\\n│  1. Context researcher      │\\n│  2. Ambiguity detector      │\\n│  3. Requirement-state graph │\\n│  4. Question selector       │\\n│  5. Human Q/A               │\\n│  6. Spec critic             │\\n└──────────────┬──────────────┘\\n               │\\n       Intent Contract vN\\n               │\\n        ┌──────┴──────┐\\n        ▼             ▼\\n holdout builder   synthetic-user\\n invariant maker    scenario maker\\n        │             │\\n        └──────┬──────┘\\n               │\\n               ▼\\n          coding agent\\n               │\\n               ▼\\n         verification\\n               │\\n          ┌────┴────┐\\n          │         │\\n       resolved   semantic\\n                  ambiguity\\n                     │\\n                     └────► Intent Broker\\n```\\n\\nThe critical part is that **the coding agent doesn't decide when the requirements are sufficiently specified**.\\n\\nThe Intent Broker does.\\n\\n### 1. Research before bothering the human\\n\\nIt should first inspect everything machine-discoverable:\\n\\n```text\\nrepository\\nexisting requirements\\nissues\\ncurrent implementation\\ntests\\narchitecture\\nhistorical decisions\\nknown bugs\\nrelevant FOSSIL knowledge\\nexternal technical documentation\\n```\\n\\nThen separate:\\n\\n```text\\nKNOWN\\nDISCOVERABLE\\nHUMAN-ONLY\\nCONFLICTING\\nUNKNOWN\\n```\\n\\nThe human should not be asked where a file lives or what the current API does.\\n\\nHuman attention is reserved for things like:\\n\\n\u003e Should correction destroy previous state or preserve history?\\n\\n\u003e Should this operation fail closed or make a best effort?\\n\\n\u003e Is compatibility more important than latency here?\\n\\nThose are authority questions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Maintain an explicit requirement-state graph\\n\\nThis is where I think OntoAgent provides the most useful research direction.\\n\\nOntoAgent doesn't just free-chat. It constructs an **experience ontology** of requirement concerns, scores which concerns are relevant, reranks them and chooses the next elicitation question. Its reported experiments improved elicitation effectiveness by 33% and question efficiency by 21% over its tested baselines. citeturn104105academia2\\n\\nFor engineering I'd use a concern graph such as:\\n\\n```text\\nFeature\\n├── user-visible behavior\\n├── state transitions\\n├── error behavior\\n├── boundaries\\n├── persistence\\n├── history\\n├── permissions\\n├── concurrency\\n├── compatibility\\n├── migration\\n├── performance\\n├── rollback\\n├── observability\\n├── non-goals\\n└── acceptance\\n```\\n\\nBut each concern has state:\\n\\n```text\\nconfirmed\\ninferred-high-confidence\\ninferred-low-confidence\\nunknown\\nconflicting\\nnot-applicable\\ndeferred-to-prototype\\n```\\n\\nNow the agent knows **what it does not know**.\\n\\nThat's much better than another long prose requirements document.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Select questions based on value, not completeness\\n\\nI would explicitly optimize something like:\\n\\n```text\\nquestion priority =\\n    probability assumption is wrong\\n  × damage if wrong\\n  × cost of discovering it later\\n  × architectural irreversibility\\n  ÷ human effort\\n```\\n\\nYou don't need the math to be perfectly calibrated initially.\\n\\nThe principle matters.\\n\\nGATE provides useful evidence here: instead of merely asking generic questions, LMs generated informative questions and edge cases to elicit preferences. Users reported lower effort than writing prompts/examples themselves, and the process surfaced considerations they had not originally thought of. citeturn899256academia0\\n\\nSo instead of asking:\\n\\n\u003e “What are your temporal semantics?”\\n\\nthe broker should generate:\\n\\n\u003e You told the system on Monday that the project uses SQLite. On Tuesday you correct that to Postgres. Six months later an agent asks what database the project currently uses. Should it answer only Postgres while keeping SQLite as historical state, or erase SQLite entirely?\\n\\nThat is a much higher-information human interaction.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 4. The answers become requirement atoms\\n\\nSomething like:\\n\\n```yaml\\nid: REQ-042\\n\\nscope:\\n  feature: project-memory\\n\\nbehavior:\\n  explicit_correction_changes_current_state: true\\n  preserve_prior_value_as_history: true\\n\\nauthority:\\n  source: human\\n  confirmation_turn: INTENT-17\\n\\nimportance: critical\\n\\nacceptance:\\n  - current query returns corrected value\\n  - historical query returns prior value\\n  - superseded value never leaks as current\\n\\nunresolved: []\\n```\\n\\nNow Codex isn't interpreting a chat.\\n\\nIt receives:\\n\\n```text\\nREQ-042\\nREQ-043\\nREQ-044\\n...\\n```\\n\\nplus examples and non-goals.\\n\\nAnd these can go into FOSSIL with provenance linking the human dialogue → requirement → decision → tests → implementation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5. There must be an adaptive **STOP gate**\\n\\nThis is important because otherwise we create the requirements equivalent of analysis paralysis.\\n\\nThe broker should stop asking questions when:\\n\\n```text\\nno unresolved high-impact ambiguity\\nAND\\nrequired behavior has observable acceptance criteria\\nAND\\ncritical failure semantics are known\\nAND\\nscope/non-goals are bounded\\nAND\\nremaining unknowns are explicitly safe to defer\\n```\\n\\nNot:\\n\\n\u003e “We asked 10 questions.”\\n\\nAnd not:\\n\\n\u003e “The model feels confident.”\\n\\nThe same research that supports clarification also warns us that clarification is currently difficult. ClarifyCodeBench explicitly measures both important questions being asked too late and unnecessary interaction. citeturn104105academia1\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 6. Critique the requirements *before* coding\\n\\nThis should be a separate agent again.\\n\\nGive it:\\n\\n```text\\nrequirements\\nrepo\\narchitecture\\nhistorical incidents\\n```\\n\\nand tell it only:\\n\\n\u003e Find assumptions, contradictions, missing boundary cases and cases where two requirements cannot simultaneously hold.\\n\\nThis is not trivial. SpecBench evaluates specification-level reasoning against critiques historically raised by expert maintainers in Kubernetes, React, Rust, TVM and vLLM. Its best evaluated agent achieved only 44.4% accuracy. citeturn899256academia1\\n\\nSo we should not assume the first elicitation agent can also reliably certify its own specification.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7. Freeze some tests *before implementation*\\n\\nThen take confirmed requirements and independently generate:\\n\\n```text\\npublic examples\\nproperties/invariants\\nhidden scenarios\\nsynthetic-user trajectories\\nboundary cases\\nmutation targets\\n```\\n\\nThe implementer gets the specification but not the entire hidden test set.\\n\\nThis becomes important because coding agents can optimize visible tests without satisfying intended composed behavior. The 2026 systems-level SpecBench found frontier agents could saturate visible validation while still exhibiting substantial failure on held-out composed tests, with the gap worsening on longer tasks. citeturn899256academia2\\n\\nSo the intent artifact becomes the common root for both:\\n\\n```text\\nimplementation\\nAND\\nindependent evaluation\\n```\\n\\ninstead of implementation creating its own definition of success.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Has anyone actually implemented this?\\n\\n**Pieces: yes. The complete system we're describing: I did not find a mature public implementation of the whole thing.**\\n\\nThe closest examples I found are:\\n\\n- **Ask-or-Assume**: a real research implementation using OpenHands + Claude Sonnet 4.5 that separates uncertainty/underspecification handling from execution and demonstrated the improvement mentioned above. citeturn104105academia0\\n- **OntoAgent**: an implemented research prototype for structured requirements interviewing using an ontology rather than free-form clarification chat. citeturn104105academia2\\n- **GATE**: an implemented active task-elicitation research framework using generated questions and edge cases to learn user preferences efficiently. citeturn899256academia0\\n- **GitHub Spec Kit**: probably the closest currently available practical open-source workflow. It has explicit `specify → clarify → plan → checklist → tasks → analyze → implement → converge` phases and supports many coding agents, including Codex, Claude-style tools and others. Its `/speckit.clarify` stage explicitly resolves underspecified areas and folds answers back into the specification before planning. citeturn195515search2turn195515search3\\n\\nSpec Kit is particularly interesting because it is **already productized**, not merely a paper. It also has extensions, integrations, versioned/pinned components and workflows. citeturn195515search7\\n\\nBut I would **not simply adopt Spec Kit as the final answer**.\\n\\nIts clarification stage is optional in the documented workflow, whereas the evidence we're discussing suggests that for materially ambiguous/high-risk tasks it should be a **gate**. citeturn195515search3 And the stronger system we're describing would add things not captured by ordinary spec-driven development:\\n\\n```text\\nautomatic ambiguity risk scoring\\nstructured concern graph\\nhuman-attention budget\\nexplicit authority distinctions\\nadaptive stopping\\nindependent spec critic\\npre-implementation hidden holdouts\\nsynthetic-user fuzz generation\\nfeedback into requirements\\nFOSSIL provenance\\n```\\n\\nThat combination is the interesting research/build opportunity.\\n\\n## I'd call it an **Intent Compiler**\\n\\nBecause its job is analogous to a compiler:\\n\\n```text\\nmessy human intent\\n        │\\n      parse\\n        │\\n  detect ambiguity\\n        │\\n resolve semantics\\n        │\\n type/check consistency\\n        │\\n produce diagnostics\\n        │\\n        ▼\\nversioned executable specification\\n```\\n\\nAnd importantly:\\n\\n```text\\ncompile error:\\nREQUIREMENT_AMBIGUOUS\\n\\ncompile error:\\nCONFLICTING_REQUIREMENTS\\n\\ncompile warning:\\nASSUMPTION_DEFERRED\\n\\ncompile success:\\nREADY_FOR_IMPLEMENTATION\\n```\\n\\nThen the coding agent gets the compiled artifact.\\n\\nThat seems much stronger than trying to train or prompt every future coding agent to magically develop excellent requirements-engineering instincts.\\n\\nAnd the latest empirical evidence makes the need unusually concrete: underspecified coding agents frequently **guess instead of asking**; UnderSpecBench recently measured boundary violations in 55.8–67.8% of runs under underspecified DevOps instructions across tested Codex/Claude Code/OpenCode configurations. citeturn899256academia3\\n\\nSo I would make **“no implementation until intent compilation reaches READY”** a harness-level rule for sufficiently risky or ambiguous work, not an etiquette suggestion in `AGENTS.md`.\",{\"_1287\":1288},{\"_454\":1542,\"_461\":2094,\"_47\":2095,\"_465\":2096,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":2097,\"_474\":475},\"8eb7f0a0-0d57-4851-8679-30e17218a565\",[1516],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":2101},1786907621.492767,{\"_1279\":1305,\"_465\":2100},{\"_1296\":1297,\"_1298\":744,\"_1299\":2098,\"_1301\":2099,\"_1303\":1304,\"_493\":494,\"_495\":1556,\"_497\":498,\"_499\":1557,\"_501\":1557,\"_490\":60,\"_502\":60,\"_59\":60,\"_503\":504,\"_505\":14,\"_506\":2092,\"_507\":1558,\"_509\":26,\"_510\":1559,\"_512\":10},1786907597.6843348,1786907621.458789,\"Worked for 23s\",{},{\"_454\":2092,\"_461\":2105,\"_47\":2106,\"_465\":2107,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":2108,\"_474\":475},\"9e25b4c6-9d31-4c