"_664\":1149,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":-5,\"_2635\":2725,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2726,\"_1130\":2727,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},\"[2404.16045] Elicitron: An LLM Agent-Based Simulation Framework for Design Requirements Elicitation\",\"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-1/D7RZubq_Y-MitldZwQiirXN3UOsATR6AOqrIUk5bWVbMv5KXZHAEj7wAQsT2919TRwXtaKzDCOCEAySR3jLTEmL06lKW5wl49TjF4j_vuOUu_9Ky_m9nDGhslVeJVBTl\",[],[2728],{\"_665\":970,\"_667\":2622,\"_669\":844},\"citeturn565739academia2\",7702,7728,[2400,2401],[],\"([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07338?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[2737],[],{\"_46\":2738,\"_34\":2401,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":2739,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2740,\"_1130\":2741,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},\"Intent Mismatch Causes LLMs to Get Lost in Multi-Turn Conversation\",1770435664,[],[2742],{\"_665\":962,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":1047},\"citeturn565739academia0\",7523,7549,[2395,2396],[],\"([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[2751],[],{\"_46\":2752,\"_34\":2396,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":2753,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2754,\"_1130\":2755,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},\"Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts\",1688666051,[],[2756],{\"_665\":962,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":666},\"citeturn684258view0turn684258view1\",5875,5913,[1590],[],\"([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11589))\",[2765],[],{\"_46\":2766,\"_34\":1590,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":-5,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2767,\"_1130\":2768,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},\"[2310.11589] Eliciting Human Preferences with Language Models\",[2771],[2769,2770],{\"_665\":970,\"_667\":2622,\"_669\":666},{\"_665\":970,\"_667\":2622,\"_669\":471},{\"_46\":2766,\"_34\":1590,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":-5,\"_664\":1149},4659,4681,[1600],[],[2778],[],{\"_46\":2028,\"_34\":1600,\"_664\":2118,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":-5,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2779,\"_1130\":2780,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[2781],{\"_665\":966,\"_667\":2622,\"_669\":666},\"citeturn937688academia0\",4216,4242,[1594,1595],[],[2789],[],{\"_46\":1973,\"_34\":1595,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":1974,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2790,\"_1130\":2791,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[2792],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":1188,\"_669\":666},\"citeturn644966view0\",3000,3022,[2398],[],\"([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.13069))\",[2801],[],{\"_46\":2552,\"_34\":2398,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":-5,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2802,\"_1130\":2803,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[2804],{\"_665\":844,\"_667\":2622,\"_669\":666},\"citeturn684258view2\",2009,2031,[2405],[],\"([arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.11240))\",[2813],[],{\"_46\":2814,\"_34\":2405,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":-5,\"_2635\":2815,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2816,\"_1130\":2817,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},\"[2605.11240] When to Ask a Question: Understanding Communication Strategies in Generative AI Tools\",\"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-1/eOe38nZqV_QfnqxKEBjax7B0_X4r_zx9_UgsuxZH3gJTfjvTlvDJUzL5xF49BUPDXVulls-RDug4K7Rxjh3F607Z6juLCIxNVAXORsfomEm-CH3SKXlMMdBQVDCCQWuw\",[],[2818],{\"_665\":970,\"_667\":2622,\"_669\":1047},1249,1271,[2398],[],[2825],[],{\"_46\":2552,\"_34\":2398,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":-5,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2826,\"_1130\":2827,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[2828],{\"_665\":844,\"_667\":2622,\"_669\":666},853,875,[1600],[],[2835],[],{\"_46\":2028,\"_34\":1600,\"_664\":2118,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":-5,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":2836,\"_1130\":2837,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[2838],{\"_665\":966,\"_667\":2622,\"_669\":666},[1278],[2841],\"Yes. After looking at the newer research, I think your dissatisfaction can be stated much more precisely:\\n\\n\u003e **The coding agent is being asked to solve the wrong problem too early.**\\n\u003e\\n\u003e It is optimized to turn text into code, while the first problem is actually to turn incomplete human intent into a sufficiently explicit behavioral contract.\\n\\nAnd the evidence says this is **not automatically fixed by using a smarter coding model**.\\n\\nRecent ClarifyCodeBench results are particularly relevant: strong coding ability and strong clarification ability are decoupled. More reasoning improved code generation much more than ambiguity detection, and clarification performance degraded sharply as tasks contained multiple ambiguities. The benchmark specifically penalizes both asking important questions too late and wasting unnecessary interaction rounds. citeturn776196view0\\n\\nAmbig-SWE found essentially the same failure from another direction: models often fail to recognize that a software task is underspecified and default to proceeding without interaction. Yet when interaction was actually enabled and used effectively, task performance improved substantially—up to 74% over the non-interactive underspecified condition in their experiments. citeturn644966view0\\n\\nSo I think **“ask better questions before coding” deserves to be an engineered subsystem**.\\n\\n## But I would modify one part of your hypothesis\\n\\nI don't think *everything* is ultimately solvable by Q/A + tests.\\n\\nThe human may not know what they want until they see a prototype. Preferences can conflict. Requirements can change after using the product. Two stakeholders can legitimately want incompatible things. Some consequences are only discoverable in production.\\n\\nThere is also a fundamental tradeoff: asking more questions can improve preference representation, but excessive questioning itself burdens the user. Recent theoretical and empirical work explicitly models that tradeoff rather than assuming “more questions = better.” citeturn684258view2\\n\\nSo the objective should not be:\\n\\n\u003e Ask until everything is known.\\n\\nIt should be:\\n\\n\u003e **Spend the minimum amount of scarce human attention needed to eliminate high-impact ambiguity.**\\n\\nThat is a much stronger objective.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Human attention becomes the expensive compute\\n\\nSuppose frontier inference is nearly free.\\n\\nThen I'd establish this resource hierarchy:\\n\\n```text\\ncheap:\\n    model inference\\n    repository search\\n    documentation search\\n    code execution\\n    simulation\\n    fuzzing\\n    mutation\\n    static analysis\\n\\nmoderate:\\n    CI / benchmark compute\\n    live testing\\n\\nexpensive:\\n    HUMAN ATTENTION\\n```\\n\\nThat means an agent should **never ask the human something it can reliably discover itself**.\\n\\nAmbig-SWE actually observed agents wasting interaction turns asking for navigational information such as file locations that could have been recovered from the repository, sometimes rigidly re-exploring the same information even after the user supplied it. citeturn644966view0\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n\u003e “Which file implements this?”\\n\\nis usually a bad human question.\\n\\nBut:\\n\\n\u003e “When the user corrects a project fact, should the previous value remain historically queryable or be treated as deleted?”\\n\\nis an excellent human question.\\n\\nThe former consumes human time to obtain **machine-discoverable information**.\\n\\nThe latter resolves a **product-semantic choice only the stakeholder has authority to make**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# The interviewer should therefore be a different agent\\n\\nI would not rely on:\\n\\n```text\\nCodex:\\n  ask questions\\n  understand intent\\n  design\\n  implement\\n  test\\n  review\\n```\\n\\nThat's too much epistemic coupling.\\n\\nInstead:\\n\\n```text\\nHuman\\n  │\\n  ▼\\nIntent / Requirements Agent\\n  │\\n  ▼\\nRequirement State\\n  │\\n  ├──────► independent specification critics\\n  │\\n  ├──────► hidden-test generators\\n  │\\n  └──────► synthetic user generators\\n  │\\n  ▼\\nCoding Agent\\n```\\n\\nThere is research support for making elicitation structured instead of free-form. OntoAgent, for example, builds an explicit ontology of requirements concerns and uses it to decide which concern to ask about next; the authors report improvements in both elicitation effectiveness and question efficiency over their baselines. citeturn937688academia0\\n\\nThat is much closer to what you're describing than a normal chatbot.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# What should the Q/A actually be doing?\\n\\nClarifyCodeBench's ambiguity taxonomy gives us a surprisingly useful starting point. Their annotated software ambiguities include things such as behavior, terminology, edge cases, ranges, ordering/atomicity, output format, comparison/tie rules, units, collection semantics and numerical precision. citeturn776196view0\\n\\nWe can extend that for real engineering:\\n\\n```text\\nbehavior\\nfailure behavior\\nstate/lifecycle\\npersistence\\nhistorical semantics\\nsecurity/trust\\npermissions\\nperformance expectations\\nmigration\\ncompatibility\\nrollback\\ndata loss tolerance\\nUI/user experience\\nscope/non-goals\\nexternal integrations\\nedge conditions\\n```\\n\\nThe requirements agent maintains which dimensions are:\\n\\n```text\\nKNOWN\\nINFERABLE\\nUNKNOWN_LOW_IMPACT\\nUNKNOWN_HIGH_IMPACT\\nCONFLICTING\\nDEFERRED_TO_PROTOTYPE\\n```\\n\\nThen it asks only about **high-value unresolved items**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# The question selector itself should optimize information value\\n\\nInstead of an LLM thinking:\\n\\n\u003e “What sounds like a useful next question?”\\n\\ngive it an objective approximately like:\\n\\n```text\\nquestion_value =\\n    ambiguity\\n  × impact_if_wrong\\n  × irreversibility\\n  × uncertainty\\n  × inability_to_test_later\\n  ÷ human_effort\\n```\\n\\nThis is conceptually aligned with active elicitation work. GATE showed that models can generate informative questions and edge cases that elicited more useful preference information than ordinary user-written prompting or labels, while participants reported lower effort and surfaced considerations they had not initially anticipated. citeturn684258view0turn684258view1\\n\\nThat suggests an especially powerful interaction technique:\\n\\nDon't always ask abstract questions.\\n\\nSometimes generate **contrasting concrete examples**.\\n\\nInstead of:\\n\\n\u003e “What are your persistence semantics?”\\n\\nask:\\n\\n```text\\nSuppose this happens:\\n\\nMonday:\\n  You tell FOSSIL \\\"Project X uses SQLite.\\\"\\n\\nTuesday:\\n  You correct it: \\\"We migrated to PostgreSQL.\\\"\\n\\nFriday:\\n  An agent asks:\\n  \\\"What database does Project X use?\\\"\\n\\nShould it:\\n\\nA. Answer PostgreSQL only.\\nB. Answer PostgreSQL, but preserve SQLite as historical state.\\nC. Delete all traces of SQLite.\\nD. Something else.\\n```\\n\\nThat is much easier for a human to answer accurately.\\n\\nAnd the answer is immediately testable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# The Q/A should produce executable requirement atoms\\n\\nNot another giant prose specification.\\n\\nSomething conceptually like:\\n\\n```yaml\\nrequirement_id: REQ-017\\n\\nstatement:\\n  Current queries must return PostgreSQL after\\n  an explicit user correction.\\n\\nhistorical_behavior:\\n  SQLite remains queryable as historical state.\\n\\nsource:\\n  human-confirmed\\n\\nimportance:\\n  critical\\n\\nstate:\\n  confirmed\\n\\nderived_invariants:\\n  - current_state != superseded_state\\n  - history preserves previous accepted value\\n\\nacceptance_examples:\\n  - scenario-41\\n\\nunresolved:\\n  []\\n```\\n\\nNow something important happens.\\n\\n**The conversation stops being the requirement.**\\n\\nThe conversation produced the requirement.\\n\\nThat helps context rot enormously.\\n\\nLong-context research has repeatedly shown that merely giving a model a large history does not guarantee robust use of all relevant information; important information can be underused depending on its position. citeturn565739academia0 More recent work specifically attributes multi-turn degradation partly to intent mismatch and explores separating intent mediation from task execution. citeturn565739academia2\\n\\nSo every new ephemeral agent should get the compact current requirement state, not 300 turns of chat.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# And now your 1,000 synthetic-user idea becomes much stronger\\n\\nBecause now the synthetic users are **not inventing the intended behavior**.\\n\\nThe human-confirmed requirements already define that.\\n\\nSynthetic agents are trying to find situations where the implementation violates them.\\n\\n```text\\nHuman-confirmed requirements\\n          │\\n          ▼\\n scenario generator\\n    /     |      \\\\\\n persona  state   adversarial\\n fuzz     fuzz    fuzz\\n    \\\\     |      /\\n          ▼\\n  1,000+ trajectories\\n          │\\n          ▼\\n behavioral oracle\\n          │\\n      PASS / FAIL\\n```\\n\\nElicitron provides some evidence that large populations of simulated LLM users can expose diverse and even latent product needs. citeturn684258view3\\n\\nBut I would use those discoveries differently.\\n\\nIf synthetic user #634 discovers:\\n\\n\u003e “What if the person changes projects halfway through the session?”\\n\\nthat does **not automatically become a requirement**.\\n\\nIt becomes:\\n\\n```text\\nNEW_UNCOVERED_SCENARIO\\n```\\n\\nThe system then asks:\\n\\nCan the existing requirements determine the answer?\\n\\nIf yes → test it automatically.\\n\\nIf no and impact is low → record/defer.\\n\\nIf no and impact is high → **ask the human one focused question**.\\n\\nThat's how 1,000 simulated humans can **save** human time instead of generating 1,000 things for the human to review.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# This gives us a very different engineering lifecycle\\n\\nI would research/build something like this:\\n\\n1. **Automatic context acquisition.** Agents inspect repository, existing docs, previous decisions, historical bugs and external technical facts first. Humans aren't used as search engines.\\n\\n2. **Ambiguity mapping.** A dedicated requirements agent turns the request into explicit knowns, unknowns, assumptions, conflicts and authority questions. Clarification research suggests this must itself be separately evaluated because current coding capability does not imply clarification capability. citeturn776196view0turn644966view0\\n\\n3. **Information-efficient Q/A.** Ask one or a small batch of high-value questions, often using concrete alternatives, counterexamples or tiny prototypes. Measure useful-question recall, redundant-question rate and human minutes—not merely number of turns. ClarifyCodeBench's TKQR/ORA metrics are directly relevant precedent. citeturn776196view0\\n\\n4. **Requirement checkpoint.** Answers become versioned requirement atoms, invariants, examples, non-goals and explicit unresolved assumptions. ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148 treats requirements engineering as an iterative lifecycle process rather than merely an initial prompt-writing exercise. citeturn781367search0\\n\\n5. **Independent specification attack.** Before coding, other models try to find omissions, contradictory requirements and impossible combinations. This is important because even frontier agents remain weak at specification-level critique: the recent SpecBench reports its best evaluated agent at only 44.4% accuracy against historical expert-maintainer specification critiques. citeturn781367academia24\\n\\n6. **Freeze part of the oracle before implementation.** Generate properties, acceptance scenarios and hidden holdouts from confirmed requirements. The coding agent does not get to redefine them.\\n\\n7. **Implement a bounded phase.** Coding becomes downstream of a much clearer contract.\\n\\n8. **Attack the implementation.** Run normal tests, mutations, fuzzing, synthetic-user trajectories, failure injection, temporal scenarios and independent critics. LLM-assisted iterative mutation testing has shown stronger fault detection than direct one-shot test generation in experimental work. citeturn684258view6\\n\\n9. **Escalate only semantic uncertainty to the human.** A thousand synthetic interactions might generate 80 failures, cluster into six root causes, automatically resolve five using existing requirements, and ask the human **one new question** about the remaining ambiguity.\\n\\n10. **Only then start the next phase.** The new requirement state, minimized counterexamples and verified outcomes survive; the disposable agent conversations do not.\\n\\nThat is considerably different from today's:\\n\\n```text\\nprompt\\n→ plan\\n→ write 40 files\\n→ tests pass\\n→ human review\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Agent-written tests are specifically not enough\\n\\nRecent evidence strengthens your criticism here.\\n\\nA 2026 empirical study of software agents found that simply increasing or decreasing how often agents wrote their own tests did not significantly change final resolution outcomes in its controlled experiments; many agent-written tests served more as observational/debug feedback than strong assertion-based verification. citeturn340414academia0\\n\\nAnother 2026 mutation-driven study found LLM-generated tests could initially show strong line/branch coverage while degrading substantially as program semantics or even syntax evolved, suggesting sensitivity to surface form and weak regression understanding. citeturn684258view5\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n\u003e “Codex implemented it and Codex wrote tests that pass”\\n\\nshould be weak evidence.\\n\\nWhereas:\\n\\n\u003e “Codex implemented it; independently frozen behavioral properties survived hidden tests, mutants, simulated-user state transitions and externally observed behavior”\\n\\nis much stronger.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# The most interesting research problem may actually be the interviewer\\n\\nNot the coder.\\n\\nI would define success for such an **Intent Agent** as something like:\\n\\n```text\\nmaximize:\\n  downstream requirement correctness\\n  important ambiguity resolved\\n  downstream testability\\n  avoided rework\\n\\nminimize:\\n  human minutes\\n  redundant questions\\n  machine-discoverable questions\\n  premature implementation\\n  late requirement surprises\\n```\\n\\nAnd its most important metric may be:\\n\\n\u003e **How many expensive human corrections were required after implementation that could reasonably have been surfaced before implementation?**\\n\\nThat's much closer to your actual dissatisfaction than pass@1 on SWE-bench.\\n\\nThe remarkable thing is that very recent research is moving toward almost exactly this missing layer: interactive ambiguity benchmarks, structured requirements interviews, active task elicitation and specification-level evaluation. But the results also suggest the capability is currently weak enough that **we should engineer it as a workflow with memory, taxonomies, external checks and measured human-cost objectives rather than trust a frontier model to naturally behave this way.** citeturn776196view0turn644966view0turn937688academia0\\n\\nAnd that connects all the pieces you've been bringing up—ephemeral specialist agents, fuzzing, mutation, holdouts, formal invariants, synthetic humans and context-rot control—around one central rule:\\n\\n\u003e **Human intent becomes a versioned engineering artifact before code becomes an implementation artifact.**\\n\\nCheap inference is then used aggressively everywhere **around** scarce human judgment: research first, ask fewer better questions, generate counterexamples, attack assumptions, simulate users, test mutants and compress failures back into the smallest possible question that genuinely requires the human.\\n\\nI can also monitor new research specifically on **interactive requirements/clarification for coding agents and agentic verification** as this area is moving quickly.\\n\\ngenui{\\\"suggest_automation\\\":{\\\"label\\\":\\\"Track agent intent research\\\"}}\",{\"_1287\":1288},{\"_454\":2335,\"_461\":2846,\"_47\":2847,\"_465\":2848,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":2849,\"_474\":475},\"3a1f2339-8214-4200-813e-293789c29a02\",[2319],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":2854},1786906904.374653,{\"_1279\":1305,\"_465\":2853},{\"_1296\":1297,\"_1298\":2850,\"_1299\":2851,\"_1301\":2852,\"_1303\"