70},\"citeturn392568search0turn392568search5\",3388,3430,[4014,4015,4016,4017],[],[4404],[],{\"_46\":4280,\"_34\":4017,\"_664\":4281,\"_663\":4326,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":4405,\"_1130\":4406,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[4409],[4407,4408],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":666},{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":941},{\"_46\":4339,\"_34\":4015,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_664\":4281},\"citeturn392568search3turn392568search6\",3048,3090,[4018,4019,4024,4025],[],[4417],[],{\"_46\":4282,\"_34\":4019,\"_664\":4283,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":4418,\"_1130\":4419,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[4422],[4420,4421],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":844},{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":966},{\"_46\":4179,\"_34\":4025,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_664\":4283},2024,2048,[4026,4027],[],[4429],[],{\"_46\":4074,\"_34\":4027,\"_664\":4284,\"_663\":4077,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":4430,\"_1130\":4431,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[4432],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":970},\"citeturn392568search1turn392568search2\",1043,1085,[4020,4021,4022,4023],[],[4440],[],{\"_46\":4285,\"_34\":4021,\"_664\":4286,\"_663\":4243,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":4441,\"_1130\":4442,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[4445],[4443,4444],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":471},{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":1047},{\"_46\":4253,\"_34\":4023,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_664\":4286},[1278],[4448],\"Yes. The historical record gives us something much more useful than a list of enterprise patterns: **a set of failure-tested design principles**.\\n\\nThe deepest lesson is:\\n\\n\u003e **Scale is not primarily the ability to add features. Scale is the ability to keep changing, replacing, migrating and deleting things after your original assumptions turn out to be wrong.**\\n\\nThat is the standard I would use for FOSSIL.\\n\\n### 1. Assume our first module boundaries will be wrong\\n\\nShopify is unusually instructive here. They originally organized a huge application into understandable business domains and created Packwerk to enforce the dependency graph. Later they found that some of those human-friendly domain boundaries did **not** correspond to how the software actually executed; enforcing the imagined architecture sometimes introduced awkward indirection and made code harder to understand. They eventually distinguished top-level domains used for human organization from smaller **functional packages** that are the real runtime dependency units. citeturn392568search1turn392568search2\\n\\nThat means we should **not freeze FOSSIL's folder taxonomy as ontology**.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```text\\ndomain/\\nfeature/\\nfoundation/\\n```\\n\\ncan help humans navigate, but the real architectural unit should be whatever can actually:\\n\\n```text\\nrun independently\\nhave explicit dependencies\\nhave a bounded public contract\\nbe tested independently\\nbe replaced independently\\n```\\n\\nAnd we need to allow boundaries themselves to be refactored.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Stable interfaces matter more than stable implementations\\n\\nUber gives us probably the strongest historical example. They ended up with roughly **2,200 microservices**, then grouped them into about **70 domains** because direct dependence on thousands of individual services created enormous migration pressure. Uber reported a microservice half-life of roughly **1.5 years**; their solution was stable domain gateways so underlying implementations could churn without forcing every caller to migrate. citeturn392568search4\\n\\nThat suggests this FOSSIL shape:\\n\\n```text\\n                Retrieval contract\\n                       │\\n              stable for callers\\n                       │\\n        ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐\\n        │              │              │\\n       BGE          Qwen v2       future thing\\n    incumbent        shadow\\n```\\n\\nnot:\\n\\n```text\\nwhole system imports BGE implementation\\n```\\n\\nBut it also tells us **not to create 100 interfaces**.\\n\\nThe interface should exist where we actually expect implementation churn.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. The unit we should design around is the **migration**, not the plugin\\n\\nThis is probably the biggest change I would make to our thinking.\\n\\nGoogle has had to build dedicated large-scale migration infrastructure because even with a shared repository, updating APIs, frameworks and data types across billions of lines of code becomes a major engineering activity. They use dependency discovery, static analysis and large-scale transformation infrastructure specifically for this. citeturn392568search3turn392568search6\\n\\nKubernetes similarly treats evolution as an explicit lifecycle: multiple API representations can coexist, versions must round-trip without losing information, preferred/storage versions advance in stages, and an old stored representation cannot simply become unreadable because the API moved on. citeturn392568search0turn392568search5\\n\\nSo whenever we say:\\n\\n\u003e “This is pluggable.”\\n\\nwe should actually ask:\\n\\n```text\\nCan I introduce replacement B\\nwhile A is running?\\n\\nCan A and B coexist?\\n\\nCan I compare them?\\n\\nCan existing data be read by B?\\n\\nCan I roll back B → A?\\n\\nCan I migrate stored data?\\n\\nCan I remove A completely afterward?\\n```\\n\\nIf the answer is no, it isn't really replaceable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4. **Removal** should be a first-class architectural operation\\n\\nMost frameworks optimize for:\\n\\n```text\\ninstall plugin\\n```\\n\\nLarge systems eventually suffer because nobody optimized:\\n\\n```text\\nuninstall plugin\\n```\\n\\nUber's “migration hell” arose precisely because implementation churn propagated outward to large numbers of consumers. citeturn392568search4 Kubernetes's lengthy deprecation rules exist because removing something that other systems/data still depend on is much harder than introducing it. citeturn392568search0\\n\\nSo I would give FOSSIL explicit architecture tests for:\\n\\n```text\\nADD\\nREPLACE\\nDUAL-RUN\\nROLL BACK\\nMIGRATE\\nREMOVE\\n```\\n\\n**Remove** is especially valuable.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n\u003e Delete Graphiti completely.\\n\\nIf FOSSIL's canonical corpus still works, the graph rebuilds when another projection is selected, no imports point to Graphiti internals, and no canonical IDs depend on Neo4j, then we genuinely have a replaceable graph implementation.\\n\\nThat's far more meaningful than putting Graphiti in `/plugins`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 5. Don't confuse static architectural compliance with real isolation\\n\\nShopify learned this painfully.\\n\\nPackwerk could report a package with no dependency violations, yet when Shopify tried actually **running the package by itself**, additional hidden dependencies appeared through initialization, loading and runtime behavior that the static checker couldn't see. Getting one foundational package genuinely isolated required additional boot tests, runtime tests and months of work. citeturn392568search1\\n\\nThat's an extremely important lesson for us.\\n\\nA FOSSIL module shouldn't count as isolated because:\\n\\n```text\\nmanifest says isolated\\nimports check passes\\n```\\n\\nIt should count as isolated because:\\n\\n```text\\nmanifest valid\\n+\\ndependency test passes\\n+\\nimport architecture passes\\n+\\nmodule boots independently\\n+\\nmodule conformance tests pass\\n+\\nsystem works when alternative is substituted\\n```\\n\\nSo **manifest = claim**.\\n\\nTests = evidence.\\n\\nThat's actually very FOSSIL-like.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 6. Don't let the architecture tooling become the architecture\\n\\nShopify also found an especially relevant failure: Packwerk's privacy mechanism started influencing folder layouts and API design in ways the original tool wasn't intended for. Developers created public structures because the tool made them convenient, not necessarily because they were good APIs. Shopify ultimately removed that privacy feature. citeturn392568search1\\n\\nThat is a direct warning for our manifest idea.\\n\\nWe should not end up with:\\n\\n```text\\nmanifest requires category\\n       ↓\\nengineer invents category\\n       ↓\\ncode gets contorted to satisfy manifest\\n```\\n\\nThe manifest should describe reality and allow CI to check selected invariants.\\n\\nIf the actual code repeatedly disagrees with the architecture model, **we should consider the possibility that the architecture model is wrong**.\\n\\nNot automatically force the code into compliance.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 7. Human organizational boundaries and machine dependency boundaries are different\\n\\nThis Shopify lesson is subtle and excellent.\\n\\nHumans like:\\n\\n```text\\nBilling\\nCheckout\\nShipping\\nResearch\\nKnowledge\\n```\\n\\nMachines care about:\\n\\n```text\\nA imports B\\nB persists C\\nC invokes D\\n```\\n\\nThose are not necessarily the same hierarchy. Shopify ended up using broader components/domains for organizational comprehension while smaller packages represented real dependency units. citeturn392568search1\\n\\nFor FOSSIL this could mean:\\n\\n```text\\nHuman/domain view\\n\\nKnowledge\\nResearch\\nEngineering\\nSource Evidence\\n```\\n\\nwhile underneath:\\n\\n```text\\nExecutable/package view\\n\\nidentity\\nevent-log\\nlifecycle\\npack-access\\nsource-snapshot\\nretrieval\\nprojection\\ncontext\\n```\\n\\nA project/domain pack is a **knowledge boundary**.\\n\\nA Python package/module is a **code dependency boundary**.\\n\\nThose don't have to mirror each other.\\n\\nThat's important.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 8. Separate API versions from storage versions\\n\\nKubernetes learned this at enormous scale.\\n\\nThe external object representation and the actual persisted storage representation are deliberately separate. Clients can interact using supported API versions while the server converts to/from the chosen storage representation. citeturn392568search5\\n\\nThat is highly applicable to FOSSIL.\\n\\nInstead of:\\n\\n```text\\nclaim-v1 file\\nclaim-v2 file\\nclaim-v3 file\\n\\nevery consumer somehow handles all three\\n```\\n\\nwe probably want something conceptually like:\\n\\n```text\\nclaim API v1 ─┐\\nclaim API v2 ─┼──\u003e canonical model ──\u003e storage representation\\nclaim API v3 ─┘\\n```\\n\\nwith explicit conversions.\\n\\nThis could prevent exactly the future problem you described:\\n\\n\u003e “Why is this data schema different from that one, and which one should the agent use?”\\n\\nThere should be **one component responsible for answering that question**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 9. Compatibility needs overlap, not flag-day upgrades\\n\\nKubernetes does not simply declare:\\n\\n\u003e tomorrow everything is v2.\\n\\nThe system supports an overlapping period where old and new representations coexist; only later can the preferred/storage representation move forward. citeturn392568search0\\n\\nThat's the pattern I would use for FOSSIL feature replacement:\\n\\n```text\\nV1 active\\n   ↓\\nV2 introduced experimental\\n   ↓\\nV1 + V2 shadow\\n   ↓\\nV2 canary\\n   ↓\\nV2 preferred\\n   ↓\\nold data migrated\\n   ↓\\nV1 deprecated\\n   ↓\\nV1 removed\\n```\\n\\nThis connects directly to the shadow/canary idea you raised earlier.\\n\\nIt's not an exotic AI concept.\\n\\nIt's a mature software evolution pattern.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 10. A shared source of truth is extremely valuable\\n\\nGoogle's monorepo research emphasizes having a common source of truth for a huge developer population. The model has substantial tooling costs, but a major benefit is that dependencies and usages are discoverable centrally, enabling cross-codebase changes and migrations. citeturn392568search3\\n\\nFor us, the lesson isn't necessarily “monorepo everything.”\\n\\nIt's:\\n\\n\u003e **Don't make architectural truth require tribal knowledge.**\\n\\nA future human or agent should be able to mechanically discover:\\n\\n```text\\nmodule\\nowner\\npublic contract\\ndependencies\\nconsumers\\nactive implementation\\nalternative implementations\\ndata/schema versions\\nmigration status\\ntests\\ndeprecation status\\n```\\n\\nThat is where a small verified manifest/catalog has enormous value.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# What I think this means for the architecture\\n\\nRather than a giant plugin framework, I think we want **three separate things**.\\n\\n### 1. A very small durable kernel\\n\\nThings whose semantics should change rarely:\\n\\n```text\\nidentity\\ndurable events\\nevidence/source identity\\nlifecycle\\npack boundaries\\nprovenance\\n```\\n\\nChanging these is a schema/protocol migration, not installing a plugin.\\n\\n### 2. Explicit replaceable ports\\n\\nThings experience tells us are likely to churn:\\n\\n```text\\nstorage\\nretrieval\\nreranking\\nprojection\\ncontext construction\\npossibly admission/promotion policy later\\n```\\n\\nEach port gets:\\n\\n```text\\ntyped contract\\nbehavioral conformance tests\\ndefault implementation\\nimplementation identity\\n```\\n\\n### 3. An engineering evolution substrate\\n\\nThis is the part enterprises eventually discover they need:\\n\\n```text\\ndependency graph\\nmodule ownership\\narchitecture checks\\nschema registry\\nmigration registry\\nimplementation registry\\ndeprecation lifecycle\\nshadow/canary comparison\\nconformance suites\\nwork/change receipts\\n```\\n\\nImportantly, this third layer should **help the architecture evolve**.\\n\\nIt should not decide what the architecture must always be.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# And we can derive a very useful invariant\\n\\nI'd propose this as the architectural north star:\\n\\n\u003e **No important implementation, representation, model, database, or feature should be permanent merely because removing it became too expensive.**\\n\\nIf something deserves permanence, it should be because its **semantic contract** deserves permanence.\\n\\nNot because 87 callers import its private classes.\\n\\nThat is the lesson behind Uber's gateways, Kubernetes's conversion/version policies, Google's migration infrastructure and Shopify's dependency-boundary work. citeturn392568search4turn392568search0turn392568search6turn392568search1\\n\\nAnd one more:\\n\\n\u003e **Architecture should optimize for recovering from bad architectural decisions.**\\n\\nBecause history strongly suggests that some of our current decisions **will be wrong**.\\n\\nIf we can split a module, merge two modules, replace Graphiti, change claim representation, introduce a new retrieval strategy, run it beside the old one, migrate the corpus, roll back, and eventually delete the old implementation **without rewriting FOSSIL**, then we've actually built for scale.\\n\\nThat is a much stronger goal than “pluggable architecture.”\",{\"_1287\":1288},{\"_454\":3950,\"_461\":4453,\"_47\":4454,\"_465\":4455,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":4456,\"_474\":475},\"f0d146bd-c6ea-48c9-951a-175242fbf452\",[3930],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":4460},1786905809.451332,{\"_1279\":1305,\"_465\":4459},{\"_1296\":1297,\"_1298\":782,\"_1299\":4457,\"_1301\":4458,\"_1303\":1304,\"_493\":494,\"_495\":3964,\"_497\":498,\"_499\":3965,\"_501\":3965,\"_490\":60,\"_502\":60,\"_59\":60,\"_503\":504,\"_505\":14,\"_506\":4451,\"_507\":3966,\"_509\":26,\"_510\":3967,\"_512\":10},1786905793.492311,1786905809.43875,\"Worked for 15s\",{},{\"_454\":4451,\"_461\":4464,\"_47\":4465,\"_465\":4466,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":4467,\"_474\":475},\"f1137a30-060e-4abf-b7e2-9c71c9869e82\",[3950],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":4514},1786905809.447864,{\"_1279\":1376,\"_1376\":4510,\"_1378\":4511},{\"_1315\":4468,\"_1296\":1317,\"_1318\":1319,\"_1320\":4469,\"_1322\":1323,\"_1299\":4457,\"_493\":494,\"_1324\":1325,\"_495\":3964,\"_497\":498,\"_499\":3965,\"_501\":3965,\"_490\":60,\"_502\":60,\"_59\":60,\"_503\":504,\"_505\":14,\"_506\":4462,\"_507\":3966,\"_509\":26,\"_510\":3967,\"_512\":10},{\"_483\":4475,\"_1332\":4476},[4470,4471,4472,4473,4474