611,5612,5613],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":666},{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":1047},{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":957},{\"_46\":4189,\"_34\":4188,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_664\":4283},{\"_46\":5217,\"_34\":4648,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_664\":5478},\"citeturn495121search1\",3087,3111,[4020,4021],[],[5623],[],{\"_46\":4285,\"_34\":4021,\"_664\":4286,\"_663\":4243,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":5624,\"_1130\":5625,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[5626],{\"_665\":1047,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":471},\"citeturn495121search4turn495121search6\",2705,2747,[4658,4659,4022,4023],[],[5634],[],{\"_46\":4253,\"_34\":4023,\"_664\":4286,\"_663\":4256,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":5635,\"_1130\":5636,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[5639],[5637,5638],{\"_665\":1047,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":970},{\"_665\":1047,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":966},{\"_46\":5134,\"_34\":4659,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_664\":4286},2265,2289,[4660,4661],[],[5646],[],{\"_46\":4074,\"_34\":4661,\"_664\":4284,\"_663\":4077,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":5647,\"_1130\":5648,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[5649],{\"_665\":471,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":471},\"citeturn453647search0turn453647search1\",1552,1594,[4660,4661,4662,4663],[],\"([Uber](https://www.uber.com/us/en/blog/service-oriented-architecture/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[5658],[],{\"_46\":5479,\"_34\":4663,\"_664\":4284,\"_663\":4072,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":5659,\"_1130\":5660,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[5663],[5661,5662],{\"_665\":471,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":666},{\"_665\":471,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":471},{\"_46\":4074,\"_34\":4661,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_664\":4284},\"citeturn453647search0turn416624search12\",946,989,[4635,4636,4662,4663],[],[5671],[],{\"_46\":5479,\"_34\":4663,\"_664\":4284,\"_663\":4072,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":5672,\"_1130\":5673,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[5676],[5674,5675],{\"_665\":471,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":666},{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":936},{\"_46\":4936,\"_34\":4636,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_664\":3584},[1278],[5679],\"The interesting answer is that the biggest companies **did not solve this once**. Their internal software repeatedly went through a cycle:\\n\\n\u003e **simple system → growth → coupling → decomposition → too much decomposition → standardization/platforms → stronger domain boundaries → continuous migration**\\n\\nAnd some of the strongest companies ended up surprisingly close to what you're thinking about—but only **after discovering where naïve modularity failed**.\\n\\n1. **They usually began with a monolith, because that was initially the correct architecture.** Uber started with one Python application and one PostgreSQL database; as products, cities, teams and deployment frequency grew, changing one area increasingly affected everything else. Amazon similarly describes its move away from a monolithic architecture as being driven not only by traffic but by organizational sluggishness and the need for teams to own and evolve things independently. citeturn453647search0turn416624search12\\n\\n2. **Then many companies decomposed aggressively—and discovered that microservices don't remove coupling, they relocate it.** Uber eventually had more than 2,000 microservices. Their engineers found that simple features could cross many services and teams, dependencies became difficult to trace, migrations became painful, and independently deployed services could form what was effectively a “networked monolith.” Earlier, with only 500+ services, Uber was already reporting that simply finding the right service and understanding its contract was difficult. citeturn453647search0turn453647search1\\n\\n3. **The correction was to organize around larger stable domains, not individual tiny services.** Uber introduced Domain-Oriented Microservice Architecture: roughly 2,200 services were being grouped into about 70 domains, domains exposed gateways, layers constrained dependency direction, and explicit extension points were created for product-specific logic/data. Critically, Uber reports that core services churn rapidly—their measured microservice “half-life” was about 1.5 years—so consumers depending directly on individual services caused migration hell. Stable domain gateways allowed underlying services to be rewritten without migrating hundreds of consumers. citeturn453647search1\\n\\n4. **Other companies deliberately did not take the microservice route.** Shopify had a huge Rails monolith and concluded that splitting code into services solely to enforce boundaries would convert a code-design problem into a distributed-systems problem. Instead it progressively converted the monolith into components/packages and created Packwerk so CI mechanically rejects illegal cross-boundary dependencies. citeturn495121search4turn495121search6 Interestingly, Shopify later discovered that some of its original domain boundaries were wrong: gigantic dependency-violation lists showed that the architecture did not match how the code actually behaved. It subsequently distinguished human-oriented domain components from smaller functional packages that form the real dependency units. citeturn495121search1\\n\\n5. **At enormous scale, companies often centralize source while modularizing dependencies.** Google famously keeps billions of lines of code in a common repository; one benefit engineers report is being able to discover APIs, centrally manage dependencies, and automatically migrate dependent code when an API changes. Meta likewise chose to make its source-control/build tooling scale rather than split code merely because source-control tooling had scaling limits. citeturn416624search0turn416624search2turn416624search11 Meta's Buck/Buck2 story is particularly relevant: when creating modules required piles of boilerplate, engineers rationally avoided modules and dumped more code into existing ones. Meta responded by making module declaration cheap and dependency relationships explicit. citeturn416624search17turn416624search5\\n\\n6. **Eventually “what exists, who owns it, and how do I use it?” becomes its own infrastructure problem.** Spotify created what became Backstage because engineers increasingly struggled to answer questions like which service/API to use, which version was current, where documentation lived, and who owned something. The resulting software catalog separates `Domain`, `System`, `Component`, `API`, resources and organizational ownership, with metadata commonly stored beside source code and harvested into a centralized view. citeturn495121search13turn495121search14turn495121search15\\n\\n7. **Data/schema evolution becomes infrastructure, not team convention.** Large event ecosystems commonly use registries that mechanically reject incompatible schemas. Confluent Schema Registry, for example, supports backward, forward, full and transitive compatibility modes and explicitly links the chosen compatibility policy to the required producer/consumer upgrade order. citeturn467228search1 Kubernetes goes even further: API representation version and persisted storage version are separate concepts, old persisted data must remain decodable/convertible, and moving the preferred/storage API forward requires an overlapping release that understands both old and new representations. citeturn467228search5\\n\\n8. **Replacement is usually gradual and dual-run, not “delete old thing, install plugin.”** This is where modern Meta has an especially useful example. Meta spent years escaping a divergent internal WebRTC fork by keeping the upstream system as a stable skeleton and injecting Meta-specific implementations at selected component boundaries. During migration they could build two variants simultaneously for A/B testing while continuously taking upstream upgrades. citeturn416624search19 That pattern—stable skeleton, narrow injection seams, old/new coexistence, measurement, then retirement—is much safer than a generic override mechanism.\\n\\nThe important lesson is therefore **not** “enterprises use microservices.”\\n\\nIt's this:\\n\\n```text\\n                 STABLE SYSTEM MODEL\\n                         │\\n            ┌────────────┼─────────────┐\\n            │            │             │\\n         Domain A     Domain B      Domain C\\n            │\\n        stable gateway\\n            │\\n      ┌─────┼──────────┐\\n      │     │          │\\n   impl A  impl B   experimental B2\\n                    (shadow/canary)\\n\\nmetadata/catalog knows:\\n  owner\\n  contract\\n  consumers\\n  schema versions\\n  active implementation\\n  migration state\\n  tests\\n```\\n\\n### What evolved was not just their application architecture\\n\\nThe mature internal stack increasingly contains **two different systems**.\\n\\nThe first is the actual product:\\n\\n```text\\nbusiness/domain code\\nstorage\\nAPIs\\nservices\\nfeatures\\n```\\n\\nThe second is a **change-management substrate**:\\n\\n```text\\nsource/build graph\\nownership\\nAPI contracts\\nschema registry\\nservice catalog\\nCI/conformance tests\\ndeployment system\\nobservability\\nmigration tooling\\nfeature flags / experiments\\n```\\n\\nThat's a crucial observation for FOSSIL.\\n\\nYou don't necessarily want FOSSIL's *product kernel* to implement every future extensibility scenario.\\n\\nYou want enough **engineering substrate surrounding it** that a future engineer can safely ask:\\n\\n```text\\nWhat owns retrieval?\\n\\nWhat contract must a replacement implement?\\n\\nWho consumes that contract?\\n\\nWhich implementation is active?\\n\\nCan two implementations run together?\\n\\nWhat data does it own?\\n\\nWhich schema versions exist?\\n\\nWhat transforms v1 → v2?\\n\\nCan I shadow-run my candidate?\\n\\nWhat benchmark determines promotion?\\n\\nHow do I rollback?\\n\\nHow do I remove the old implementation completely?\\n```\\n\\nThose are essentially the institutional capabilities Uber, Meta, Google, Shopify and Spotify had to build once raw code modularity stopped being sufficient. citeturn453647search1turn416624search20turn495121search15\\n\\n### And there is an important warning for your idea\\n\\nUber is almost a direct historical experiment for what you're proposing.\\n\\nThey first gained:\\n\\n\u003e lots of independently changeable little things.\\n\\nThen that became:\\n\\n\u003e thousands of little things nobody could reason about globally.\\n\\nTheir answer was **not more microservices**.\\n\\nIt was:\\n\\n\u003e **domains + gateways + dependency layers + ownership + constrained extension points.**\\n\\nciteturn453647search1\\n\\nShopify reached a related conclusion from the opposite direction:\\n\\n\u003e **keep one deployable system where possible, but mechanically enforce internal package boundaries.**\\n\\nciteturn495121search4\\n\\nSo for FOSSIL, I would lean toward a **domain-oriented modular monolith first**, not a microservice/plugin universe.\\n\\nSomething closer to:\\n\\n```text\\nFOSSIL\\n│\\n├── kernel\\n│   identity / events / evidence / lifecycle\\n│\\n├── domains\\n│   corpus\\n│   sources\\n│   knowledge\\n│   packs\\n│   research\\n│\\n├── ports\\n│   storage\\n│   projection\\n│   retrieval\\n│   context\\n│\\n└── adapters\\n    filesystem / R2\\n    Graphiti\\n    BGE / BM25\\n```\\n\\nwith a small machine-readable catalog explaining ownership/contracts/versions, plus CI enforcing the dependency graph.\\n\\nThen later, if `retrieval` genuinely needs five implementations, **that seam becomes richer**. If `lifecycle` never needs substitution, we don't invent a plugin platform for it.\\n\\nThat is much closer to how large systems actually seem to survive: **stable abstractions emerge from repeated change; implementation churn happens behind them; tooling makes the boundaries enforceable; and migrations are expected to be continuous rather than exceptional.** citeturn453647search1turn495121search1\\n\\nThe strongest enterprise lesson for your concern may be: **design for migration, not for permanence.** A scalable architecture isn't one where the original modules turn out to be correct forever. It's one where discovering that a boundary or representation was wrong is survivable.\",{\"_1287\":1288},{\"_454\":4607,\"_461\":5684,\"_47\":5685,\"_465\":5686,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":5687,\"_474\":475},\"91e8429b-dd06-4aff-98ea-504a876f19dd\",[4591],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":5691},1786905722.587479,{\"_1279\":1305,\"_465\":5690},{\"_1296\":1297,\"_1298\":910,\"_1299\":5688,\"_1301\":5689,\"_1303\":1304,\"_493\":494,\"_495\":4621,\"_497\":498,\"_499\":4622,\"_501\":4622,\"_490\":60,\"_502\":60,\"_59\":60,\"_503\":504,\"_505\":14,\"_506\":5682,\"_507\":4623,\"_509\":26,\"_510\":4624,\"_512\":10},1786905695.1442087,1786905722.3667545,\"Worked for 27s\",{},{\"_454\":5682,\"_461\":5695,\"_47\":5696,\"_465\":5697,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":5698,\"_474\":475},\"0551b015-a879-41d9-aa2d-0fc8badf4535\",[4607],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":5785},1786905722.562235,{\"_1279\":1376,\"_1376\":5781,\"_1378\":5782},{\"_1315\":5699,\"_1296\":1317,\"_1318\":1319,\"_1320\":5700,\"_1322\":1323,\"_1299\":5688,\"_493\":494,\"_1324\":1325,\"_495\":4621,\"_497\":498,\"_499\":4622,\"_501\":4622,\"_490\":60,\"_502\":60,\"_59\":60,\"_503\":504,\"_505\":14,\"_506\":5693,\"_507\":4623,\"_509\":26,\"_510\":4624,\"_512\":10},{\"_483\":5703,\"_1332\":5704},[4472,5701,5702],\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=engineering.fb.com\u0026sz=128\",\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=aws.amazon.com\u0026sz=128\",[5708,5709,5710],[5705,5706,5707],\"fb7b9871-39a1-4989-8f45-59301ab83857\",\"bb71f6cc-e0c3-4c8c-a329-add24fac5d00\",\"89ba12a0-a828-443b-b078-245903334cd3\",{\"_43\":650,\"_651\":4143,\"_653\":5746},{\"_43\":650,\"_651\":5722,\"_653\":5723},{\"_43\":650,\"_651\":5711,\"_653\":5712},\"aws.amazon.com\",[5713,5714,5715],{\"_43\":656,\"_34\":4635,\"_46\":4936,\"_659\":1132,\"_661\"