ioning/020-introduction.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[7232],[],{\"_46\":7233,\"_34\":5954,\"_664\":7163,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":7234,\"_1130\":7235,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},\"OSGi Semantic Versioning - Introduction | OSGi Docs\",[],[7236],{\"_665\":471,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":666},\"citeturn306808search0\",7409,7433,[4016,4017],[],[7244],[],{\"_46\":4280,\"_34\":4017,\"_664\":4281,\"_663\":4326,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":7245,\"_1130\":7246,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[7247],{\"_665\":970,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":666},\"citeturn837510search1turn837510search10\",7186,7229,[5959,4122,5960,4131],[],\"([Kubernetes](https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/extend-kubernetes/custom-resources/custom-resource-definition-versioning/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[7256],[],{\"_46\":4123,\"_34\":4122,\"_664\":4281,\"_663\":1742,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":7257,\"_1130\":7258,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[7261],[7259,7260],{\"_665\":471,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":471},{\"_665\":471,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":1040},{\"_46\":4132,\"_34\":4131,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_664\":4281},\"citeturn983283search9\",5914,5938,[5949,5950],[],\"([Bazel Documentation](https://bazel.build/concepts/visibility?hl=en\u0026utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[7270],[],{\"_46\":6368,\"_34\":5950,\"_664\":1144,\"_663\":6354,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":7271,\"_1130\":7272,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[7273],{\"_665\":844,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":831},\"citeturn983283search2turn983283search3\",4800,4842,[5955,5956,5965,5966],[],\"([Python documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[7282],[],{\"_46\":6677,\"_34\":5956,\"_664\":7159,\"_663\":7283,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":7284,\"_1130\":7285,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},1784592000,[7288],[7286,7287],{\"_665\":844,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":1047},{\"_665\":844,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":844},{\"_46\":6688,\"_34\":5966,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_664\":7289},\"Typing Documentation\",\"citeturn983283search1\",3130,3154,[5963,5964],[],\"([Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[7298],[],{\"_46\":7160,\"_34\":5964,\"_664\":7161,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":7299,\"_1130\":7300,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[7301],{\"_665\":844,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":471},\"citeturn837510search4\",2458,2482,[5951,5952],[],\"([OSGi Docs](https://docs.osgi.org/whitepaper/semantic-versioning/010-executive-summary.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[7310],[],{\"_46\":7162,\"_34\":5952,\"_664\":7163,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":7311,\"_1130\":7312,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[7313],{\"_665\":471,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":970},\"citeturn461032search0\",945,969,[5957,5958],[],\"([DOI](https://doi.org/10.1145/361598.361623?utm_source=chatgpt.com))\",[7322],[],{\"_46\":7164,\"_34\":5958,\"_664\":7165,\"_663\":6277,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":7323,\"_1130\":7324,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[7325],{\"_665\":666,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":666},[1278],[7328],\"Yes. I think your **problem statement is stronger than the architecture sketch we were converging on**.\\n\\nThe problem is not “how do I make FOSSIL modular?” It is:\\n\\n\u003e **How do I keep the marginal cost of adding, replacing, removing, or experimentally changing a capability or data representation low as the system grows—without humans or agents needing tribal knowledge of the whole codebase?**\\n\\nThat is worth designing for. But there are several ways your current idea could accidentally make that problem *worse*.\\n\\n## The main criticism: too many extension points can destroy extensibility\\n\\nParnas’s classic modularity result is often simplified to “use modules,” but his actual point is more important: **the criterion used to choose the module boundary determines whether modularity helps.** Modules should hide design decisions that are likely to change independently; merely splitting a system into components does not deliver the benefit. citeturn461032search0\\n\\nThat directly challenges a generic:\\n\\n```text\\nbase/\\nfoundation/\\nfeatures/\\nplugins/\\n```\\n\\ntaxonomy.\\n\\nThose names describe **architectural status**, not necessarily **reasons for change**.\\n\\n`foundation/` in particular has a serious risk of becoming:\\n\\n\u003e “important things that aren't features.”\\n\\nEventually storage helpers, registries, configuration, migrations, observability, context, security, schemas and plugin machinery all end up there.\\n\\nYou have then created another monolith, just one directory lower.\\n\\nI would therefore **not make base/foundation/feature the primary module decomposition**.\\n\\nUse it as maturity metadata if useful. The real decomposition should follow **capability and ownership seams**:\\n\\n```text\\nstorage\\nretrieval\\ncontext\\nprojection\\nknowledge-lifecycle\\nsource-evidence\\npack-boundary\\nengineering-preflight\\n```\\n\\nEach should hide a decision that can plausibly change independently.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## “Everything pluggable” creates a compatibility tax\\n\\nThis is the biggest danger in your plan.\\n\\nOnce something becomes an official plugin interface, it stops being an implementation detail.\\n\\nIt becomes an API you have to preserve.\\n\\nOSGi is one of the longest-running serious module/plugin ecosystems, and its versioning guidance makes an important distinction: an API can have **consumers** and **providers/implementers**, and changes that remain safe for consumers can still break implementers. Their guidance explicitly requires different compatibility ranges for those roles. citeturn837510search4\\n\\nSo suppose FOSSIL has 30 pluggable interfaces:\\n\\n```text\\nRetriever\\nReranker\\nContextProvider\\nProjection\\nAdmissionPolicy\\nPromotionPolicy\\nSourceResolver\\nMigrationProvider\\nStorage\\nPackResolver\\n...\\n```\\n\\nYou have created **30 compatibility promises**.\\n\\nNow every evolution requires asking:\\n\\n```text\\nDoes v2 break old plugins?\\nCan v1 plugin run against core v3?\\nCan two versions coexist?\\nWho migrates plugin-owned data?\\nWhat happens if plugin A requires contract v1\\nbut plugin B requires contract v2?\\n```\\n\\nSemantic Versioning exists largely because dependency graphs produce exactly this kind of version-lock/version-promiscuity problem as systems grow. citeturn983283search1\\n\\nSo my recommendation is almost the reverse:\\n\\n\u003e **Make only high-volatility, independently substitutable decisions pluggable. Keep everything else private until substitution is actually needed.**\\n\\nYou want a *small number of excellent extension seams*, not maximum extensibility.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Arbitrary “override” is particularly dangerous\\n\\nI would remove **override existing implementation** as a general platform capability.\\n\\nThat sounds powerful:\\n\\n```text\\nfeature X overrides base Y\\n```\\n\\nbut it makes reasoning extremely difficult.\\n\\nNow the behavior of Y depends on:\\n\\n- which features are installed,\\n- registration order,\\n- project configuration,\\n- runtime scope,\\n- plugin versions,\\n- possibly precedence rules.\\n\\nFor an agent, this is brutal. Reading `retrieval.py` no longer tells it how retrieval works.\\n\\nInstead, use **explicit substitution at a composition boundary**:\\n\\n```text\\nRetrievalPort\\n     ↑\\n     |\\n ┌───┴──────────┐\\n BGE          experimental-Qwen\\n```\\n\\nand one composition root says:\\n\\n```text\\nactive retriever = experimental-Qwen\\n```\\n\\nNo feature should be able to reach into another component and patch it.\\n\\nThat produces a useful distinction:\\n\\n**extension:** add something behind an existing contract.  \\n**replacement:** select another provider of that contract.  \\n**modification:** change the contract/core.\\n\\nThose should be three different engineering operations.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Types don't solve contracts\\n\\nYour interest in strong types and import structure is justified, but there is another trap here.\\n\\nPython `Protocol` gives structural compatibility: an object is acceptable if it has the required operations with compatible types. citeturn983283search2turn983283search3\\n\\nThat proves:\\n\\n```text\\nmethod exists\\narguments fit\\nreturn type fits\\n```\\n\\nIt does **not** prove:\\n\\n```text\\nsearch respects pack boundaries\\nretrieval doesn't claim authority\\nstorage is idempotent\\nredactions don't resurrect\\nreranking preserves stable IDs\\n```\\n\\nThose are semantic contracts.\\n\\nSo every true extension point needs **both**:\\n\\n```text\\ntype/interface contract\\n+\\nbehavioral conformance suite\\n```\\n\\nA plugin is compatible only if both pass.\\n\\nThat is more important for FOSSIL than sophisticated inheritance/type machinery.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Imports should absolutely be constrained — but manifests alone are weak\\n\\nA manifest saying:\\n\\n```text\\nfeature → foundation → core\\n```\\n\\ndoesn't stop an agent from writing:\\n\\n```python\\nfrom fossil_core.internal.secret_thing import Whatever\\n```\\n\\nYou need mechanical enforcement.\\n\\nBazel's visibility system is a good example of the principle: public versus implementation dependencies are declared and builds fail when a target violates another target's visibility boundary. The explicit purpose is preserving structure as a workspace grows. citeturn983283search9\\n\\nFor Python/FOSSIL, we don't necessarily need Bazel, but we need equivalent **architecture tests**.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```text\\nbase cannot import adapters\\ncontracts cannot import implementations\\nretrieval cannot import Graphiti\\nGraphiti cannot import R2\\nfeatures cannot import private internals of sibling features\\n```\\n\\nIf CI doesn't enforce it, humans and agents will eventually violate it.\\n\\nA README is guidance.\\n\\nA manifest is declaration.\\n\\n**A failing dependency check is architecture.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Your schema concern is even more serious\\n\\nThis may become a bigger scaling problem than the code modules.\\n\\nSuppose over five years you create:\\n\\n```text\\nclaim.v1\\nclaim.v2\\nclaim.v3\\n\\nevent.v1\\nevent.v2\\n\\npack.v1\\npack.v2\\npack.v3\\n\\ncitation.v1\\ncitation.v2\\n\\nresearch.v1\\nresearch.v2\\n```\\n\\nand every component independently reads various combinations.\\n\\nYou can very quickly create a compatibility matrix.\\n\\nThe naive design:\\n\\n```text\\nevery reader understands every version\\n```\\n\\ndoesn't scale.\\n\\nKubernetes's approach is instructive. A resource may have multiple **served representations**, but there is one designated **storage version**; conversion handles other API versions. Old stored objects are deliberately migrated before an old version is finally removed. citeturn837510search1turn837510search10\\n\\nThe stronger Kubernetes API policy also requires conversion/round-tripping between simultaneously supported versions and treats version retirement as an explicit staged process. citeturn306808search0\\n\\nThat suggests something better for FOSSIL:\\n\\n```text\\n             API representation v1\\n                    ↓\\n                 convert\\n                    ↓\\nCANONICAL INTERNAL REPRESENTATION\\n                    ↑\\n                 convert\\n                    ↑\\n             API representation v2\\n```\\n\\nand independently:\\n\\n```text\\nstorage schema v3\\n```\\n\\nDo **not** let each application layer invent how it interprets old schemas.\\n\\nOne migration/conversion subsystem owns that problem.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## And don't version everything\\n\\nOur previous conversation drifted toward:\\n\\n```text\\nschema_version\\ncontract_version\\nimplementation_version\\npolicy_version\\nmodule_version\\nmanifest_version\\nontology_version\\n...\\n```\\n\\nAll of those *can* be legitimate.\\n\\nBut if you independently version every internal structure, the metadata itself becomes cognitive load.\\n\\nVersion things when compatibility matters across a boundary:\\n\\n- persisted durable data;\\n- published API/contracts;\\n- external plugin interface;\\n- independently deployed component;\\n- reproducibility-critical model/policy.\\n\\nDon't assign a lifecycle-managed compatibility version to an internal helper structure that gets changed atomically with its callers.\\n\\nOSGi's experience is relevant here: versioning is powerful precisely because it is attached to explicitly evolving exported artifacts. citeturn837510search0\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Another criticism: don't create a plugin system before you have plugins\\n\\nThis is where scale architecture projects often go off the rails.\\n\\nYou could spend months building:\\n\\n```text\\nregistry\\ndiscovery\\nmanifest parser\\ndependency resolver\\noverride resolver\\nversion negotiation\\ncompatibility matrix\\nplugin lifecycle\\nmigration API\\nmodule graph\\n```\\n\\nbefore anyone has added the second retriever.\\n\\nThat is a lot of infrastructure whose requirements are hypothetical.\\n\\nThe agentic-software research actually reinforces being conservative here. Agentless showed that a deliberately simple localization → repair → validation workflow could outperform substantially more elaborate autonomous-agent scaffolds on its evaluated benchmark. Its point was not “agents are bad”; it was that extra orchestration has a real complexity cost and must justify itself. citeturn116101academia0\\n\\nLikewise, SWE-agent found that **simple, well-designed interfaces to the repository and execution environment materially affect agent performance**. citeturn474849academia48\\n\\nSo a future-agent-friendly repository likely benefits more from:\\n\\n```text\\nobvious ownership\\nsmall APIs\\ngood names\\nlocal instructions\\nbounded dependencies\\none obvious test command\\n```\\n\\nthan from a brilliant dynamic plugin loader.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Self-validation is still a major concern\\n\\nThis applies directly to your scale goal.\\n\\nIf future agents can add modules easily, they can also add:\\n\\n```text\\nnew implementation\\n+\\nnew tests\\n+\\nnew manifest\\n+\\nnew compatibility declaration\\n```\\n\\nand tell you the module is valid.\\n\\nThat doesn't make it valid.\\n\\nCurrent coding-agent evaluation work shows why. One empirical study found plausible patches that passed benchmark validation could still exhibit materially different or incorrect behavior; another analysis found weak tests could allow suspicious patches to count as solved. citeturn474849academia50turn474849academia49\\n\\nEven OpenAI's 2026 audit of SWE-bench Verified found substantial problems in the benchmark tests themselves, including correct solutions being rejected by flawed tests. citeturn474849search1\\n\\nSo your extension architecture should separate:\\n\\n```text\\nMODULE DECLARES\\n\\\"I implement Retriever v1\\\"\\n\\n         ≠\\n\\nTYPE CHECKER SAYS\\n\\\"shape matches\\\"\\n\\n         ≠\\n\\nMODULE TESTS SAY\\n\\\"my own tests pass\\\"\\n\\n         ≠\\n\\nCORE CONFORMANCE SUITE SAYS\\n\\\"you preserve Retriever semantics\\\"\\n\\n         ≠\\n\\nREAL BENCHMARK SAYS\\n\\\"you are preferable to incumbent\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThat ladder is important.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# So I would change the architecture goal\\n\\nNot:\\n\\n\u003e **Everything should be pluggable.**\\n\\nNot:\\n\\n\u003e **Everything should fit a predefined folder.**\\n\\nNot even:\\n\\n\u003e **Everything must have a manifest.**\\n\\nInstead:\\n\\n\u003e **Every important design decision should have a known owner, and every decision expected to vary independently should have a narrow, testable replacement boundary.**\\n\\nThat is much more constrained.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# A scalable shape I'd actually test\\n\\nI would start with only something like:\\n\\n```text\\nfossil/\\n│\\n├── kernel/\\n│   ├── identity\\n│   ├── events\\n│   ├── lifecycle\\n│   ├── pack semantics\\n│   └── evidence semantics\\n│\\n├── ports/\\n│   ├── storage\\n│   ├── retrieval\\n│   ├── projection\\n│   └── context\\n│\\n├── adapters/\\n│   ├── storage/\\n│   │   ├── filesystem\\n│   │   └── s3\\n│   ├── retrieval/\\n│   │   ├── bge\\n│   │   └── bm25\\n│   └── projection/\\n│       └── graphiti\\n│\\n├── application/\\n│   ├── query\\n│   ├── ingestion\\n│   ├── promotion\\n│   └── preflight\\n│\\n├── schemas/\\n└── migrations/\\n```\\n\\nThat is closer to ports/adapters than a generic plugin platform.\\n\\nNotice what's missing:\\n\\n```text\\nplugins/\\nfeatures/\\noverrides/\\nfoundation/\\n```\\n\\nWe add those concepts only if an actual recurring need emerges.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Then define scaling tests instead of guessing\\n\\nThis is the part I think would make your idea considerably stronger.\\n\\nBefore we claim this architecture “scales,” give it adversarial change scenarios.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n**Change A:** Add a Qwen retriever.  \\nExpected: no kernel modifications; no consumer modifications.\\n\\n**Change B:** Remove Graphiti entirely.  \\nExpected: durable corpus still functions and project rebuilds with another/no projection.\\n\\n**Change C:** Introduce `claim.v2`.  \\nExpected: old durable data remains readable; explicit conversion exists; new writes use the chosen current representation.\\n\\n**Change D:** Trial a pollution filter.  \\nExpected: installed in shadow mode without modifying ingestion semantics.\\n\\n**Change E:** Remove that failed experiment.  \\nExpected: no dangling imports, data dependency, schema dependency, or config dependency.\\n\\n**Change F:** Give a fresh coding agent the repo.  \\nExpected: it can determine the owner, interface, current implementation, applicable instructions, tests, and migration constraints **without reading the whole repository**.\\n\\nThose are much better architecture acceptance tests than:\\n\\n\u003e “The directory tree looks modular.”\\n\\nAnd they make your scale goal measurable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## My current position\\n\\nYour concern is correct:\\n\\n**we absolutely should design FOSSIL so replacement and experimentation do not become progressively harder.**\\n\\nBut I'd reject three parts of the earlier direction:\\n\\n**Don't make everything pluggable.** Every plugin surface becomes an API-maintenance obligation.\\n\\n**Don't create arbitrary override machinery.** Use explicit composition/substitution through a small number of ports.\\n\\n**Don't make manifests the source of architecture truth unless CI mechanically verifies them.**\\n\\nAnd I'd add two requirements you hadn't emphasized enough:\\n\\n**Removing a feature must be a first-class test.** Extensibility that makes addition easy but deletion impossible is not modularity.\\n\\n**Migration cost must be part of every extension contract.** A replacement that swaps code but strands old durable data isn't actually replaceable.\\n\\nThat gives us a much more demanding definition of “built for scale”:\\n\\n\u003e **The architecture should minimize the blast radius of change—not maximize the number of extension mechanisms.**\\n\\nThat is the design principle I would use to review FOSSIL before adding any new architectural machinery.\",{\"_1287\":1288},{\"_454\":5875,\"_461\":7333,\"_47\":7334,\"_465\":7335,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":7336,\"_474\":475},\"c707e407-f9d8-4179-9123-b31b62bc3d1d\",[5859],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":7341},1786905552.598646,{\"_1279\":1305,\"_465\":7340},{\"_1296\":1297,\"_1298\":7337,\"_1299\":7338,\"_1301\":7339,\"_1303\":1304,\"_493\":494,\"_495\":5889,\"_497\":498,\"_499\":5890,\"_501\":5890,\"_490\":60,\"_502\":60,\"_59\":60,\"_503\":504,\"_505\":14,\"_506\":7331,\"_507\":5891,\"_509\":26,\"_510\":5892,\"_512\":10},56,1786905495.9535148,1786905552.281414,\"Worked for 56s\",{},{\"_454\":7331,\"_461\":7345,\"_47\":7346,\"_465\":7347,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":7348,\"_474\":475},\"d3789263-f459-4477-a724-af31bc88bd86\",[5875],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":7386},1786905552.350288,{\"_1279\":1376,\"_1376\":7382,\"_137