1381\":7625,\"_465\":7626,\"_1383\":7627,\"_1385\":26},{\"_1381\":7623,\"_465\":1132,\"_1383\":7624,\"_1385\":26},\"Balanced modularity risks\",[],\"Balancing modularity risks\",\"I’m researching modularity, extension boundaries, and schema evolution to test this architecture. I’ll focus on when shared foundations clarify design versus when they create coupling, versioning pain, or coordination overhead.\",[],{},{\"_454\":7610,\"_461\":7632,\"_47\":7633,\"_49\":7634,\"_465\":7635,\"_467\":468,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":7636,\"_474\":475},\"f842b59b-9644-4ea4-af3b-6ce79bb53f82\",[7599],{\"_1284\":1539,\"_472\":7644},1786905494.455709,1786905600.032987,{\"_1279\":1280,\"_1281\":7642},{\"_502\":60,\"_59\":60,\"_1422\":14,\"_483\":7637,\"_1482\":7638,\"_1525\":7639,\"_1527\":14,\"_1528\":7640,\"_1530\":14,\"_1531\":14,\"_1532\":14,\"_1533\":14,\"_1534\":7641,\"_510\":5892,\"_507\":5891,\"_495\":5889,\"_1536\":-5,\"_499\":5890,\"_501\":5890,\"_490\":60,\"_512\":10,\"_505\":14},[],[],[],[],{},[7643],\"Nono dont just agree to me pls criticize fins resources find research find scoping issue and problems\\n\\nMian goal is to build for scale, so adding removing trying new feature, trying nee data representation becomes hell , or doesnt fit anywhere or becomes impossible\",{},{\"_454\":7630,\"_461\":7648,\"_47\":7649,\"_49\":7650,\"_465\":7651,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":26,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":7652,\"_474\":475,\"_476\":477},\"93bcc647-bbb2-403b-b524-052ff8a94b64\",[7610],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":7700},1786905410.302978,1786905441.73375,{\"_1279\":1280,\"_1281\":7698},{\"_478\":7653,\"_480\":26,\"_488\":7654,\"_481\":7655,\"_486\":7656,\"_490\":60,\"_491\":7657,\"_483\":7658,\"_493\":494,\"_495\":7659,\"_497\":498,\"_499\":7660,\"_501\":7660,\"_502\":60,\"_59\":60,\"_503\":504,\"_505\":26,\"_506\":7646,\"_507\":7661,\"_509\":26,\"_510\":7662,\"_512\":10},{\"_43\":1275,\"_1276\":7697},[],[],[],{\"_513\":7663,\"_515\":7664,\"_517\":7665,\"_519\":7666,\"_521\":7667,\"_523\":7668,\"_525\":7669,\"_527\":7670,\"_529\":7671,\"_531\":7672,\"_533\":7673,\"_535\":7674,\"_537\":7675,\"_539\":7676,\"_541\":7677,\"_543\":7678,\"_545\":7679},[],\"wfr_01a00bdc700f7d2b8cfdef62158cbd84\",\"e327f91b-a931-471f-be66-aa61214e5d69\",\"saserver-centralus-prod.fck9d:conversation-turn-e327f91b-a931-471f-be66-aa61214e5d69:US\",\"conversation-turn-e327f91b-a931-471f-be66-aa61214e5d69\",{\"_454\":7696,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7695,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7694,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7693,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7692,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7691,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7690,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7689,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7688,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7687,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7686,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7685,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7684,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7683,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7682,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7681,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},{\"_454\":7680,\"_580\":14,\"_581\":14,\"_582\":-5},\"98m5p7\",\"1nbwsm\",\"tfhl73\",\"wnp22s\",\"0g490j\",\"rtx5vb\",\"izs6vf\",\"u54w6c\",\"6a4r7g\",\"w405nw\",\"jzgtti\",\"dfva5z\",\"532nri\",\"n9114a\",\"ts1vc2\",\"th4c0z\",\"xft6dq\",[1278],[7699],\"Yes — that distinction makes sense. The foundation should **anticipate extension without pre-building the extensions**.\\n\\nThe key is to design **stable extension mechanics**, not speculative features. That gives future humans and agents a clear answer to: “Where does this belong?”, “What may I replace?”, “What contract must I preserve?”, “What data version am I looking at?”, and “How do I migrate from the old implementation?”\\n\\nI’d separate it roughly like this:\\n\\n```text\\nproject/\\n│\\n├── base/\\n│   ├── ids/\\n│   ├── events/\\n│   ├── contracts/\\n│   ├── types/\\n│   └── lifecycle/\\n│\\n├── foundation/\\n│   ├── storage/\\n│   ├── retrieval/\\n│   ├── context/\\n│   ├── projection/\\n│   ├── admission/\\n│   └── migration/\\n│\\n├── features/\\n│   ├── graphiti/\\n│   ├── semantic-search/\\n│   ├── promotion-filter/\\n│   └── ...\\n│\\n├── contracts/\\n├── schemas/\\n├── migrations/\\n└── project.manifest\\n```\\n\\nThe **base layer** should be extremely boring and difficult to replace accidentally. Stable IDs, event semantics, lifecycle vocabulary, core types, capability interfaces, version identifiers. Anything in base should have a very high bar for breaking changes.\\n\\nThe **foundation layer** provides replaceable default mechanisms behind those interfaces: object storage, retrieval, projections, context construction, migration engine, etc. These should be usable out of the box but explicitly replaceable.\\n\\nThe **feature layer** is where experimentation happens. A new pollution filter, graph implementation, reranker, compression strategy, promotion policy, or local agent bridge belongs here until it earns a stronger architectural position.\\n\\nThe important part is that “pluggable” should be a **contractual property**, not merely “we put it in a plugins folder.”\\n\\nFor example, retrieval might define:\\n\\n```python\\nclass Retriever(Protocol):\\n    contract_version: Literal[\\\"fossil.retriever.v1\\\"]\\n\\n    def search(\\n        self,\\n        query: Query,\\n        scope: PackScope,\\n        limit: int,\\n    ) -\u003e list[RetrievalCandidate]:\\n        ...\\n```\\n\\nThen the default implementation could be:\\n\\n```text\\nfoundation/retrieval/bge\\n```\\n\\nwhile an experimental implementation could be:\\n\\n```text\\nfeatures/my-new-retriever\\n```\\n\\nBoth must return the same typed contract.\\n\\nA future engineer should never have to change twenty callers just to swap retrieval.\\n\\nThat also answers your **imports** concern. I would make dependency direction mechanically enforceable:\\n\\n```text\\nfeatures  → foundation → base\\n              ↓\\n           contracts\\n\\nbase NEVER imports foundation\\nbase NEVER imports features\\nfoundation NEVER imports individual feature implementations\\n```\\n\\nFeatures register themselves against contracts. The kernel/factory/registry resolves which implementation is active.\\n\\nSo instead of application code doing:\\n\\n```python\\nfrom fossil.features.graphiti import GraphitiThing\\n```\\n\\nit does:\\n\\n```python\\nprojection = registry.require(\\\"projection\\\")\\n```\\n\\nor receives the interface through dependency injection.\\n\\nThen implementation choice lives in configuration/manifest rather than being scattered through code.\\n\\nThat matters enormously for agents because otherwise an agent can implement a “plugin” correctly and unknowingly leave three hard-coded imports pointing at the previous implementation.\\n\\n### Overrides also need explicit semantics\\n\\nI think this is one of the areas most systems underspecify.\\n\\nYou want a declared precedence model such as:\\n\\n```text\\nbase contract\\n    ↓\\nfoundation default\\n    ↓\\nproject-selected implementation\\n    ↓\\noptional scoped override\\n```\\n\\nBut an override should not mean “monkey patch whatever you want.”\\n\\nIt should declare something like:\\n\\n```yaml\\nprovides:\\n  contract: fossil.retriever.v1\\n\\nimplementation:\\n  id: experimental-hybrid-v3\\n  version: 3.1.0\\n\\nreplaces:\\n  implementation: default-bge-v1\\n\\nscope:\\n  packs:\\n    - project-x\\n\\ncompatibility:\\n  input_contract: \\\"\u003e=1.0 \u003c2.0\\\"\\n  output_contract: \\\"1.x\\\"\\n\\nfallback:\\n  implementation: default-bge-v1\\n```\\n\\nNow an agent can answer:\\n\\n\u003e “This module replaces retrieval only for Project X, preserves retriever contract v1, and falls back to the default.”\\n\\ninstead of reading 30 files trying to infer it.\\n\\n### Data/schema versions need the same discipline\\n\\nThis is probably even more important than code plugins.\\n\\nNever let:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"thing\\\": \\\"...\\\"}\\n```\\n\\nsilently change meaning over time.\\n\\nEvery durable object should have a type identity and schema/version identity where appropriate:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"type\\\": \\\"fossil.claim\\\",\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": \\\"2.1\\\",\\n  \\\"id\\\": \\\"clm_...\\\",\\n  ...\\n}\\n```\\n\\nAnd migrations should be explicit transformations:\\n\\n```text\\nclaim.v1\\n   |\\nmigration m001\\n   ↓\\nclaim.v2\\n```\\n\\nwith the old schema still understandable.\\n\\nA reader should either:\\n\\n```text\\nunderstand v2\\nmigrate v1 → v2\\nor fail clearly with UNSUPPORTED_SCHEMA\\n```\\n\\nNever:\\n\\n\u003e “It looked similar enough so we parsed it.”\\n\\nThat is precisely how agents end up mixing generations of data and producing plausible nonsense.\\n\\nI would also distinguish three things that often get wrongly collapsed:\\n\\n```text\\nschema_version\\n    structure of serialized data\\n\\ncontract_version\\n    behavioral/API promise between modules\\n\\nimplementation_version\\n    actual implementation release\\n```\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```text\\nRetriever implementation:\\n    bge-local 3.2.1\\n\\nimplements:\\n    fossil.retriever.v1\\n\\nproduces:\\n    fossil.retrieval-candidate.v2\\n```\\n\\nChanging BGE internals may increment the implementation version without changing the contract.\\n\\nChanging a field in the serialized candidate may require schema v3.\\n\\nBreaking what callers may expect requires retriever contract v2.\\n\\nThose should **not** share one version number.\\n\\n### Then manifests become useful rather than bureaucratic\\n\\nA project-level manifest can provide a machine-readable architecture map:\\n\\n```yaml\\nproject: fossil-core\\n\\ncontracts:\\n  event: fossil.event.v1\\n  retriever: fossil.retriever.v1\\n  projection: fossil.projection.v1\\n\\nimplementations:\\n  storage: r2-s3-v1\\n  retriever: bge-local-v1\\n  projection: graphiti-neo4j-v1\\n\\nmodules:\\n  - base\\n  - storage\\n  - retrieval\\n  - graphiti\\n\\nschema_support:\\n  fossil.event:\\n    read: [1]\\n    write: 1\\n  fossil.claim:\\n    read: [1, 2]\\n    write: 2\\n\\nextension_policy:\\n  undeclared_modules: reject\\n  contract_override: explicit\\n```\\n\\nThat last part addresses something important in what you're saying:\\n\\n**A new piece of code should not be allowed to simply “fit wherever.”**\\n\\nIf it cannot answer:\\n\\n\u003e which module owns me?  \\n\u003e which contract do I implement?  \\n\u003e what do I depend on?  \\n\u003e what am I replacing or extending?\\n\\nthen the system can refuse registration.\\n\\nThat is much better than forcing an agent to guess architecture from directory names.\\n\\n### The guardrail I would keep\\n\\nI would still avoid making every Python file or tiny helper a “module.”\\n\\nThe module boundary should correspond to an actual **architectural substitution or ownership boundary**.\\n\\nOtherwise humans and agents drown in declarations.\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```text\\nR2 adapter       → module\\nGraphiti adapter → module\\nretrieval engine → module\\npromotion policy → module\\n\\nstring parser helper → not a module\\none serializer function → not a module\\nsmall internal class → not a module\\n```\\n\\nThat keeps the foundation powerful without turning it into enterprise-framework ceremony.\\n\\nThe long-term goal I think you're describing is very reasonable:\\n\\n\u003e **A bare, portable engineering kernel with strong types, versioned contracts, explicit ownership, one-way dependencies, declared implementations, safe overrides, migration paths, and machine-readable module boundaries — with actual product features sitting above it rather than being baked into it.**\\n\\nThat gives you foresight **without needing to know what the future features are**.\",{},{\"_454\":7646,\"_461\":7704,\"_47\":7705,\"_465\":7706,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":