s://images.openai.com/static-rsc-1/UCftQt_Dex4tdG7CKXmAvC9zMVpxEC0kQ0rRmuZGKvCI9DdKgjA_dnoAZAS77s4YYPnaf1E03rJsMEVnIN-Btzk6WRFYLiIlV4zgrye5QQY\",[],[8328],{\"_665\":941,\"_667\":2622,\"_669\":970},\"citeturn250954view0turn250954view1\",3180,3218,[7796],[],\"([Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents?via=bart41))\",[8337],[],{\"_46\":8131,\"_34\":7796,\"_664\":8275,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":-5,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":8338,\"_1130\":8339,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[8342],[8340,8341],{\"_665\":941,\"_667\":2622,\"_669\":666},{\"_665\":941,\"_667\":2622,\"_669\":471},{\"_46\":8131,\"_34\":7796,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":-5,\"_664\":8275},2966,2988,[5943],[],[8349],[],{\"_46\":7158,\"_34\":5943,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":-5,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":8350,\"_1130\":8351,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[8352],{\"_665\":970,\"_667\":2622,\"_669\":1047},\"citeturn250954view2turn585649search9\",1421,1461,[7792,7794,7795],[],\"([GitHub Docs](https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud%40latest/copilot/how-tos/configure-custom-instructions-in-your-ide/add-repository-instructions-in-your-ide?tool=vscode))\",[8361],[],{\"_46\":7967,\"_34\":7792,\"_664\":8276,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":-5,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":8362,\"_1130\":8363,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[8366],[8364,8365],{\"_665\":941,\"_667\":2622,\"_669\":1047},{\"_665\":1047,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":831},{\"_46\":7995,\"_34\":7795,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":1132,\"_664\":7157},\"citeturn905169view1\",1044,1066,[5941],[],[8374],[],{\"_46\":7206,\"_34\":5941,\"_664\":1149,\"_663\":-5,\"_659\":-5,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":8375,\"_1130\":8376,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[8377],{\"_665\":970,\"_667\":2622,\"_669\":471},\"citeturn238999search0\",718,742,[5957,5958],[],[8385],[],{\"_46\":7164,\"_34\":5958,\"_664\":7165,\"_663\":6277,\"_659\":1132,\"_1167\":-5,\"_1168\":8386,\"_1130\":8387,\"_1171\":-5,\"_1172\":-5},[],[8388],{\"_665\":844,\"_667\":668,\"_669\":666},[1278],[8391],\"Yes. The idea has a strong core, but I would **not** adopt it exactly as stated. The main danger is that an architecture invented to prevent scope explosion can itself become a **scope-management mega-framework**.\\n\\n### Where I think you are right\\n\\nA repo should expose **clear local boundaries to both humans and agents**: what a component owns, what it may mutate, what interface it exposes, how it is tested, and where its instructions apply. This is not just an AI-era idea. Parnas’s classic modularity result was that the value of modularization depends on the *criterion used for decomposition*, not simply on splitting a program into more pieces; good decomposition improves changeability and comprehensibility. citeturn238999search0\\n\\nThere is now agent-specific evidence for the same general principle. SWE-agent found that the **agent-computer interface itself materially affects coding-agent performance**—how the agent navigates repositories, edits files, and runs tests matters, rather than the model being sufficient on its own. citeturn905169view1 GitHub now supports repository-wide, path-specific, and nearest-directory `AGENTS.md` instructions, and Codex similarly scopes `AGENTS.md` by directory tree with deeper instructions taking precedence. That is strong practical support for your idea that instructions should live near the component they govern rather than in one enormous global document. citeturn250954view2turn585649search9\\n\\nSo something resembling:\\n\\n```text\\nmodule/\\n  code/\\n  tests/\\n  manifest\\n  AGENTS.md\\n  README\\n```\\n\\nis defensible.\\n\\nBut the **manifest should describe a real architectural boundary**, not create one merely because a folder exists.\\n\\n### Where I would push back hard\\n\\nYour phrase **“repeatable infrastructure for any engineering”** is where I would become suspicious.\\n\\nThe more universal the foundation tries to become, the more likely it accumulates:\\n\\narchitecture policy, agent policy, task tracking, evidence policy, promotion policy, security policy, deployment policy, RAG policy, testing policy, research policy, observability policy...\\n\\nSoon the supposedly “bare kernel” knows about everything.\\n\\nThat is exactly the opposite of information hiding.\\n\\nA better criterion is:\\n\\n\u003e **A module exists because something has an independent reason to change, an independently testable contract, or an independently replaceable implementation.**\\n\\nNot:\\n\\n\u003e “This concept seems important, therefore give it a module.”\\n\\nThis matters especially for agents because more orchestration is not monotonically better. Agentless deliberately replaced a complex autonomous-agent setup with a simple localization → repair → validation process and showed that a comparatively simple approach could outperform substantially more complicated open-source agent systems on its evaluated benchmark. The authors specifically identify complex tool interfaces, long decision trajectories, and weak self-reflection as sources of failure. citeturn905169view2 Anthropic reports a similar engineering lesson from deployed agent systems: start with the simplest solution and add agentic complexity only when the simpler approach stops being sufficient. citeturn250954view0turn250954view1\\n\\nSo I would **not** build “MVC for agents.”\\n\\nI would build something closer to a **tiny plugin kernel**.\\n\\n### The kernel should know almost nothing\\n\\nI think your portable engineering foundation should probably understand only a few primitives:\\n\\n```text\\nPROJECT\\nMODULE\\nCONTRACT\\nWORK ITEM\\nEVIDENCE / RECEIPT\\nCAPABILITY\\nACTOR\\nVERSION\\n```\\n\\nThe kernel can answer:\\n\\n```text\\nWhat module owns this?\\nWhat can it read/write?\\nWhat are its dependencies?\\nWhat contract does it expose?\\nWhat checks validate it?\\nWhat version produced this?\\nWho/what changed it?\\nWhere is the evidence?\\n```\\n\\nIt should **not understand what “good RAG retrieval,” “good database promotion,” “good UI,” “good FOSSIL knowledge,” or “good Cortex policy” means.**\\n\\nThose belong in plugins/modules.\\n\\nThat distinction is crucial.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Your `node_modules` analogy is only partly right\\n\\nThe attractive part of Node/package ecosystems is:\\n\\n```text\\nsmall public interface\\ndeclared dependency\\nexplicit version\\nreplaceable implementation\\nlocal ownership\\n```\\n\\nThose are useful.\\n\\nThe dangerous part would be reproducing **transitive-dependency sprawl** at the architecture/governance level.\\n\\nImagine an agent needs to modify one FOSSIL retriever but discovers:\\n\\n```text\\nretriever\\n→ context policy\\n→ promotion policy\\n→ evidence policy\\n→ agent methodology\\n→ project policy\\n→ shared engineering policy\\n→ universal governance policy\\n```\\n\\nNow we've built dependency hell for reasoning.\\n\\nSo I would impose an architectural rule:\\n\\n\u003e **A normal module should be understandable and changeable without loading the documentation of unrelated modules.**\\n\\nThat is arguably an even more important property for an agent than for a human developer because agent performance is highly sensitive to context selection and task scope.\\n\\nMETR's current software-task evaluations explicitly model success probability as declining with task difficulty/estimated human task duration, and note that real work with messier success criteria is harder than their clean automatically scored tasks. That argues for **bounded, well-specified work units**, not giving an agent the entire engineering constitution every time it touches a function. citeturn250954view4\\n\\n### I strongly agree with you about avoiding self-validation\\n\\nThis is probably the most important correction to the system we've been discussing.\\n\\nAn agent must not be allowed to do:\\n\\n```text\\nI wrote X.\\nI ran my test for X.\\nMy test passed.\\nTherefore X is correct.\\nTherefore promote my lesson about X.\\n```\\n\\nThat is circular.\\n\\nAnd there is empirical evidence that “tests passed” is not a sufficient correctness oracle for coding agents. A study of SWE-bench patches found cases where generated patches passed the benchmark tests despite being incorrect or incomplete; in that study, 31.08% of the inspected successful patches were classified as suspicious because of weak tests. citeturn629278view0\\n\\nSo the unit of trust cannot be:\\n\\n\u003e agent says PASS.\\n\\nNor even:\\n\\n\u003e agent's test says PASS.\\n\\nIt should be something closer to:\\n\\n```text\\nIMPLEMENTER\\n    |\\n    v\\ncandidate change\\n    |\\n    +----\u003e independent contract tests\\n    +----\u003e existing regression tests\\n    +----\u003e externally defined acceptance oracle\\n    +----\u003e benchmark / live observation where required\\n    +----\u003e human authority where judgment is required\\n                 |\\n                 v\\n            acceptance\\n```\\n\\nAnd importantly, **“independent” does not necessarily mean another LLM**.\\n\\nHaving Claude review Codex is not magically independent evidence. Two models can make the same conceptual mistake.\\n\\nIndependence should primarily come from the **oracle**:\\n\\na pre-existing test, a specification, a benchmark defined before the implementation, a live observable result, a reproducible measurement, or a human acceptance decision.\\n\\nAnother agent can be a reviewer, but it is not the proof.\\n\\n### This changes how I would treat project tracking\\n\\nYour idea that every agent's work should be separately tracked is good, but the tracker must distinguish:\\n\\n```text\\nAgent A attempted X\\nAgent A claims X succeeded\\nCI observed Y\\nHuman accepted Z\\nProduction observed Q\\n```\\n\\nThose are different facts.\\n\\nA project tracker should answer **who did what and what happened**.\\n\\nIt must not itself answer **whether the implementation is correct**.\\n\\nThat comes from evidence.\\n\\nThis separation is especially useful because SWE-agent research shows long interactive trajectories become difficult to understand and debug, while the Agentless work explicitly calls out 30–40-turn autonomous trajectories as difficult to inspect after something goes wrong. citeturn905169view2\\n\\nSo I would store **compact work receipts**, not every thought or action.\\n\\nSomething like:\\n\\n```text\\ntask_id\\nactor\\nstarting_version\\nmodule\\ndeclared_scope\\n\\nchanged:\\n  - ...\\n\\ntests_requested:\\n  - ...\\n\\ntests_observed:\\n  - test_id\\n    result\\n    external_ref\\n\\nunresolved:\\n  - ...\\n\\nterminal_status:\\n  candidate | verified | failed | blocked\\n```\\n\\nThat is auditable without turning the repository into an agent diary.\\n\\n### The module manifest should also stay tiny\\n\\nI would resist putting architecture prose into it.\\n\\nA manifest could be essentially:\\n\\n```yaml\\nmodule: retrieval\\nversion: 2\\n\\nowns:\\n  - src/retrieval/**\\n\\npublic_contract:\\n  - contracts/retrieval-v2.json\\n\\ndepends_on:\\n  - corpus-api \u003e=1 \u003c2\\n\\ncapabilities:\\n  read:\\n    - durable-corpus\\n  write:\\n    - none\\n\\ninstructions:\\n  - AGENTS.md\\n\\nverification:\\n  - tests/retrieval/**\\n  - benchmarks/retrieval-v2.yaml\\n```\\n\\nThen `AGENTS.md` explains **how to work here**.\\n\\n`README.md` explains **what this module does**.\\n\\nArchitecture/decision records explain **why it looks this way**.\\n\\nTests prove **behavior**.\\n\\nThe tracker records **work**.\\n\\nFOSSIL records **durable knowledge/evidence**.\\n\\nNone of those should impersonate another.\\n\\n### One thing I would explicitly reject\\n\\nI would reject the idea that **every concern needs a permanent module from day one**.\\n\\nInstead:\\n\\n```text\\nproblem appears\\n      ↓\\nsolve it locally\\n      ↓\\nproblem repeats / boundary becomes clear\\n      ↓\\nextract stable interface\\n      ↓\\nmodule\\n      ↓\\nprove substitutability\\n```\\n\\nnot:\\n\\n```text\\nimagine all future concerns\\n      ↓\\ncreate architecture for each\\n      ↓\\nthen start building\\n```\\n\\nThat second path is exactly the architecture-first scope explosion you were worried about five minutes ago.\\n\\n### So my critique of your proposal\\n\\nI think you're **right about locality, ownership, scoped instructions, replaceable boundaries, reproducibility, and agent provenance**.\\n\\nI think you're **at risk of overgeneralizing those ideas into a universal engineering ontology/framework before we have enough repeated failures to know what deserves standardization**.\\n\\nAnd I think the strongest correction is:\\n\\n\u003e **Don't design the foundation around agents. Design a boring software project that happens to be exceptionally legible and controllable by agents.**\\n\\nHumans should be able to navigate the same folders, read the same manifests, run the same checks, inspect the same receipts, and replace the AI worker with a human without changing the architecture.\\n\\nThat's a much healthier criterion for whether we're building durable engineering infrastructure or merely building scaffolding around today's coding agents.\\n\\nAnd for FOSSIL specifically, this argues even more strongly for what we just concluded: **finish the concrete working system first, then extract this reusable engineering substrate from the parts that demonstrably recur.** The sources support modular interfaces and scoped agent context, but they also give us substantial reason to be skeptical of extra autonomy, extra orchestration, and self-certified success. citeturn905169view1turn905169view2turn629278view0\",{\"_1287\":1288},{\"_454\":7750,\"_461\":8396,\"_47\":8397,\"_465\":8398,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":8399,\"_474\":475},\"9176cebc-5292-48d0-8e2e-fabecd15da5c\",[7730],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":8404},1786905087.305919,{\"_1279\":1305,\"_465\":8403},{\"_1296\":1297,\"_1298\":8400,\"_1299\":8401,\"_1301\":8402,\"_1303\":1304,\"_493\":494,\"_495\":7764,\"_497\