[],1462,1490,[],[],\"fileciteturn63file0L2-L2\",916,944,[],[],{\"_2294\":8917,\"_2295\":8918,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8952},{\"_2294\":8912,\"_2295\":8913,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":2299},{\"_2294\":8908,\"_2295\":8909,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8944},{\"_2294\":8904,\"_2295\":8905,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8951},{\"_2294\":8899,\"_2295\":8900,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":2300},{\"_2294\":8895,\"_2295\":8896,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8941},{\"_2294\":8891,\"_2295\":8892,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8950},{\"_2294\":8886,\"_2295\":8887,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8949},{\"_2294\":8883,\"_2295\":2773,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8949},{\"_2294\":8879,\"_2295\":1192,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8948},{\"_2294\":8874,\"_2295\":8875,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8947},{\"_2294\":8869,\"_2295\":8870,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":2300},{\"_2294\":8865,\"_2295\":8866,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8946},{\"_2294\":8860,\"_2295\":8861,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8945},{\"_2294\":8856,\"_2295\":8857,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8945},{\"_2294\":8851,\"_2295\":8852,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8944},{\"_2294\":8846,\"_2295\":8847,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8943},{\"_2294\":8841,\"_2295\":8842,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8942},{\"_2294\":8836,\"_2295\":8837,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":2299},{\"_2294\":8832,\"_2295\":8833,\"_2296\":2297,\"_2298\":8941},\"API tool citation id is not a valid file id: d5c1f5f7-3174-4dc6-907e-3e98bffbe640\",\"API tool citation id is not a valid file id: 2ced07b7-3f52-4f51-89b6-b71058532def\",\"API tool citation id is not a valid file id: 87ee6c40-0ebd-4633-bd46-51d8e6be7ae5\",\"API tool citation id is not a valid file id: 52a56ff3-2b34-48d3-9368-2f759c815381\",\"API tool citation id is not a valid file id: 56a916b1-ea26-47ec-a149-0df3c8df52e3\",\"API tool citation id is not a valid file id: ffda5a16-199e-420c-8c47-c70c505efed1\",\"API tool citation id is not a valid file id: 58fdab98-15b7-4f72-a170-69ac74fb129d\",\"API tool citation id is not a valid file id: 4b665bbb-25d7-4b18-87b0-06168391d741\",\"API tool citation id is not a valid file id: b65b681e-525b-4bcf-b944-c570ccce62ec\",\"API tool citation id is not a valid file id: 525bdd47-77c6-4db7-bf2d-56bfa296e40d\",\"API tool citation id is not a valid file id: fe1db270-3bc8-44dc-a0d7-22b1433c97ad\",\"API tool citation id is not a valid file id: 47fdc0a9-516e-4bde-8e77-10907d127d9c\",[1278],[8955],\"Yes. You’ve identified a **real missing layer**, but we should **not rewrite FOSSIL’s architecture around it yet**.\\n\\nThe existing architecture already has most of the primitives. What it does **not** yet have is a mature, executable **knowledge-admission/promotion policy** tying all of those primitives together.\\n\\n### The important distinction\\n\\nFOSSIL already separates things like this:\\n\\n```text\\nraw artifact / source\\n        ↓\\nobservation\\n        ↓\\nclaim / assumption\\n        ↓\\nevidence relationships\\n        ↓\\ndecision\\n        ↓\\nproject-local accepted knowledge\\n        ↓\\nexplicit promotion\\n        ↓\\ndomain knowledge\\n        ↓\\ncommon/general knowledge\\n```\\n\\nThat is intentional. The ontology has distinct `Evidence`, `Observation`, `Experiment`, `Claim`, `Assumption`, `Decision`, `Methodology`, etc. An experiment result is therefore **not automatically a claim**, and a decision is not automatically evidence. fileciteturn63file0L2-L2\\n\\nBut there are gaps in how rigorously we enforce the transitions between those layers.\\n\\n## 1. Is FOSSIL temporal or bitemporal?\\n\\nIt has a **bitemporal foundation**, but I would not claim it is yet a complete general-purpose bitemporal database.\\n\\nEvery durable knowledge event carries both:\\n\\n- `occurred_at` = when the represented thing actually happened;\\n- `recorded_at` = when FOSSIL learned/committed it.\\n\\nThose are exactly the two clocks you want for “what was true then?” versus “what did we know then?” queries. fileciteturn48file0L2-L2\\n\\nFOSSIL already has an `as_of_recorded_at` replay path and a real temporal benchmark. That benchmark reconstructs the corpus at different historical cutoffs and correctly tracks a previously supported SQLite decision becoming superseded while a dependent claim becomes `stale_pending_review`. fileciteturn55file0L2-L2\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n**event history:** effectively bitemporal-ready  \\n**all domain objects + query API:** not yet a fully formal bitemporal system\\n\\nI would preserve the two-clock model rather than add another temporal architecture.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. How are decisions tracked?\\n\\nThere are actually two related mechanisms.\\n\\nNormal knowledge claims have lifecycle states:\\n\\n`proposed → open → supported / disputed / rejected / superseded / retracted / stale_pending_review`\\n\\nRelations have their own lifecycle too. Dependencies can automatically become stale when a premise is superseded. fileciteturn49file0L2-L2\\n\\nResearch decisions have a separate research-state vocabulary:\\n\\n`open → provisional → accepted_for_now → rejected / superseded`\\n\\nThe research-trace contract is specifically designed to reconstruct:\\n\\n```text\\nQuestion\\n ↓\\nAlternative A ─┐\\nAlternative B ─┤\\nSources        ├─\u003e critiques\\nBenchmarks     ┤\\nAssumptions    ┤\\n               ↓\\n        Decision: provisional\\n               ↓\\n        Decision: accepted_for_now\\n               ↓\\n       implementation/commit\\n               ↓\\n       later evidence\\n               ↓\\n          superseded\\n```\\n\\nIt records alternatives, critiques, unresolved questions, source refs, claim refs, implementation refs, actors, model versions, code commit, ontology version, and research-policy version. fileciteturn58file0L2-L2 fileciteturn59file0L2-L2\\n\\nSo an agent can theoretically know:\\n\\n\u003e “This is currently our preferred architecture, but it is only provisional.”\\n\\nwithout interpreting it as proven truth.\\n\\nThat's already designed correctly.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Project vs domain vs common knowledge\\n\\nThis is one of FOSSIL's strongest existing boundaries.\\n\\nImagine:\\n\\n```text\\ncommon\\n  └─ research methodology\\n  └─ general engineering lessons\\n\\ndomain/software-engineering\\n  └─ retry semantics\\n  └─ distributed systems lessons\\n  └─ RAG evaluation methodology\\n\\nproject/fossil\\n  └─ R2 configuration\\n  └─ current Graphiti implementation\\n  └─ FOSSIL-specific failures\\n\\nproject/cortex-v5\\n  └─ V5-specific decisions\\n```\\n\\nA project can **read** mounted common/domain knowledge while only being allowed to **write into its project pack**. `PackAccess` enforces that. fileciteturn33file0L2-L2\\n\\nPromotion is explicit:\\n\\n```text\\nproject → domain\\ndomain → common\\n```\\n\\nand creates a new `knowledge.promoted` event in the target pack pointing back at the original source pack. It does not rewrite the source. fileciteturn50file0L2-L2\\n\\nThat's good.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 4. But what counts as “genuinely reusable”?\\n\\n**This is where your concern is correct.**\\n\\nWe currently have the mechanism:\\n\\n\u003e explicit promotion\\n\\nbut the promotion implementation mostly says:\\n\\n\u003e source pack, target pack, subjects, evidence refs, reason.\\n\\nIt doesn't currently contain a sufficiently rich admission policy saying:\\n\\n\u003e “Why does this deserve promotion?”\\n\\nfileciteturn50file0L2-L2\\n\\nAnd I don't think we should solve that with one dumb score like:\\n\\n\u003e confidence = 0.91 → promote.\\n\\nThat's dangerous.\\n\\nPromotion should depend on the **type of claim being promoted**.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n| Signal | What it actually proves |\\n|---|---|\\n| Unit test PASS | Behavior under that test/config |\\n| Integration test PASS | Interaction under that environment |\\n| Benchmark | Measured performance on that benchmark |\\n| Production bug | Observed failure in that circumstance |\\n| Repeated bug | Recurring failure pattern |\\n| User interaction | User-observed behavior/preference |\\n| Human acceptance | Acceptance/decision authority |\\n| External primary source | External factual evidence |\\n| Agent closeout | Report/proposal, not proof |\\n| Model agreement | Review metadata, not evidence |\\n\\nThat distinction already fits FOSSIL's ontology.\\n\\nWhat is missing is the **policy engine enforcing it during promotion**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5. Closeouts are especially important\\n\\nYour instinct here is exactly right:\\n\\n\u003e a closeout saying “this worked” must not become evidence that it worked.\\n\\nFortunately the current closeout contract partially protects this.\\n\\nA closeout has separate:\\n\\n- `outcome`\\n- `tests`\\n- `evidence`\\n- `terminal_status`\\n- `lesson_proposals`\\n\\nAnd lessons are explicitly required to be:\\n\\n```text\\nstatus: PROPOSAL\\n```\\n\\nfileciteturn51file0L2-L2\\n\\nSo this:\\n\\n```text\\nAgent closeout:\\n\\\"R2 is reliable.\\\"\\n```\\n\\nshould **not** become:\\n\\n```text\\nSUPPORTED FACT:\\nR2 is reliable.\\n```\\n\\nInstead:\\n\\n```text\\nCloseout\\n  |\\n  +--\u003e observed test: PASS\\n  |       |\\n  |       +--\u003e Actions artifact/run/digest\\n  |\\n  +--\u003e proposed lesson:\\n          \\\"R2 worked under configuration X\\\"\\n               |\\n               status = PROPOSED\\n```\\n\\nThen actual test evidence can support that bounded claim.\\n\\nEven better:\\n\\n\u003e “R2 is reliable in general”\\n\\nwould require substantially broader evidence than one successful run.\\n\\n### One gap\\n\\nI don't think the system yet has a single ingestion rule that makes this distinction **impossible to misuse across every closeout ingestion path**.\\n\\nThe schema encourages the correct behavior.\\n\\nThe future admission layer should enforce it.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 6. Human-in-the-loop is not automatically “stronger evidence”\\n\\nThis is another subtle point.\\n\\nA human saying:\\n\\n\u003e “Yes, ship this UI.”\\n\\nis extremely strong evidence for:\\n\\n\u003e “The authorized owner accepted this UI.”\\n\\nBut not necessarily evidence that:\\n\\n\u003e “This architecture has no race conditions.”\\n\\nSimilarly:\\n\\n\u003e “The user says the button is confusing.”\\n\\nis strong evidence for:\\n\\n\u003e “This user experienced confusion.”\\n\\nbut weaker evidence for:\\n\\n\u003e “All users find the button confusing.”\\n\\nSo rather than one **evidence tier**, FOSSIL already leans toward **quality dimensions**.\\n\\nSource snapshots contain separate:\\n\\n- authority\\n- directness\\n- independence\\n- reproducibility\\n- timeliness\\n\\nrather than a universal quality number. fileciteturn60file0L2-L2\\n\\nThat's a much safer foundation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7. We probably need “verification profiles,” not simple tiers\\n\\nI would add a pluggable concept like:\\n\\n```text\\nPromotionPolicy / AdmissionPolicy\\n```\\n\\nnot rewrite the ontology.\\n\\nFor example a candidate lesson could carry:\\n\\n```text\\nclaim:\\n    \\\"Retrying this R2 operation is safe\\\"\\n\\nscope:\\n    object-store adapter\\n\\nstatus:\\n    proposed\\n\\nsupport:\\n    unit_test\\n    integration_test\\n    R2_live_test\\n\\nmissing:\\n    repeated_live_observation\\n    failure-injection\\n```\\n\\nThe policy might conclude:\\n\\n```text\\nproject status: SUPPORTED\\ndomain promotion: NOT_YET_ELIGIBLE\\ncommon promotion: NOT_ELIGIBLE\\n```\\n\\nThen later:\\n\\n```text\\n3 implementations\\n+ repeated success\\n+ injected failures\\n+ no semantic regression\\n```\\n\\nmight produce:\\n\\n```text\\ndomain promotion: ELIGIBLE\\n```\\n\\nThat gives us graduated reuse **without pretending all evidence is comparable**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8. What about “best decision, but not yet verified?”\\n\\nAlready representable.\\n\\nA research run can say:\\n\\n```text\\ndecision_state = provisional\\n```\\n\\nand connect it to alternatives and evidence.\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```text\\nAlternative A\\nAlternative B\\nAlternative C\\n       ↓\\nDecision D\\nstate = provisional\\nselected_over = A,B\\nunresolved:\\n  - real production latency\\n  - failure recovery\\n```\\n\\nAfter the benchmark:\\n\\n```text\\nDecision D\\nstate = accepted_for_now\\n```\\n\\nLater:\\n\\n```text\\nDecision E SUPERSEDES Decision D\\n```\\n\\nThe old decision doesn't disappear. fileciteturn58file0L2-L2\\n\\nThat is exactly how agents should distinguish:\\n\\n**current preferred** from **proven universal truth**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9. Does everything written into FOSSIL carry authority state?\\n\\n**Not literally everything.**\\n\\nAnd this is important.\\n\\nClaims and relations do.\\n\\nResearch decisions do.\\n\\nSource snapshots have role and quality metadata.\\n\\nAgent output is explicitly candidate-only in the hardened model boundary.\\n\\nUnknown retrieved content gets demoted to `untrusted_context`.\\n\\nBut raw artifacts, observations, receipts, etc. don't all have the same claim lifecycle because they shouldn't.\\n\\nThe protection is supposed to come from **type separation**, not from assigning `supported/disputed` to every byte.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```text\\nGitHub Actions log\\n    = Artifact\\n\\n\\\"test X returned 43 ms\\\"\\n    = Observation\\n\\n\\\"system generally responds in \u003c50 ms\\\"\\n    = Claim\\n\\n\\\"therefore use architecture A\\\"\\n    = Decision\\n```\\n\\nThese should remain different objects.\\n\\nThat's much better than treating everything as a “fact.”\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10. Pollution resistance already exists at retrieval time\\n\\nThis has actually been implemented and tested.\\n\\nRetrieved content is considered **untrusted data**.\\n\\nIf a retrieved payload claims:\\n\\n```text\\nstate = supported\\n```\\n\\nFOSSIL does not trust that metadata.\\n\\nIt resolves the stable ID back against the mounted durable corpus.\\n\\nUnknown content loses claim/lifecycle/citation authority and is marked:\\n\\n```text\\nuntrusted_context\\n```\\n\\nOut-of-pack material gets dropped.\\n\\nDuplicates are collapsed.\\n\\nModels cannot turn retrieved tool instructions into executable actions.\\n\\nModel output remains `candidate_only`. fileciteturn53file0L2-L2\\n\\nThere was an adversarial eight-case poisoning benchmark and it passed the bounded suite with:\\n\\n- 0 unsupported claims\\n- exact citation correctness 1.0\\n- pack isolation 1.0\\n- candidate-only authority 1.0\\n- executable output containment 1.0\\n\\nfileciteturn54file0L2-L2\\n\\nSo **query-time pollution defense is real**.\\n\\nWhat is missing is the analogous **write-time/promotion pollution filter**.\\n\\nThat's the useful insight here.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 11. Context rot / attention rot\\n\\nPartly handled, partly not.\\n\\nAlready implemented/proven:\\n\\n- pack filtering;\\n- lifecycle filtering;\\n- lineage recovery;\\n- retrieval budgets;\\n- BGE/BM25 retrieval;\\n- deduplication of exact poisoning spam;\\n- source identity resolution;\\n- candidate-only authority;\\n- abstention;\\n- historical/current distinction;\\n- retrieval tests as corpus grows. fileciteturn54file0L2-L2 fileciteturn55file0L2-L2\\n\\nAnd an important failure was already discovered experimentally: top-k retrieval omitted a critical stale claim, causing a wrong confident answer. FOSSIL fixed it by adding deterministic lineage expansion rather than just tuning retrieval. fileciteturn56file0L2-L2\\n\\nThere is also a strong context-construction design:\\n\\n```text\\nfilter\\n→ retrieve\\n→ lifecycle/lineage resolution\\n→ select\\n→ preserve high-value spans\\n→ optionally compress\\n→ verify preservation\\n→ otherwise decompose\\n```\\n\\nwith the explicit invariant:\\n\\n\u003e filter first, compress second.\\n\\nfileciteturn52file0L2-L2\\n\\nBut that document itself says advanced compression benchmarking/implementation was still pending.\\n\\nSo **attention/context rot is recognized, not completely solved**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 12. Shadow/canary promotion testing\\n\\nThis appears to be genuinely missing.\\n\\nI found blue/green concepts for **database projections**, and extensive benchmark-before-adoption rules for retrieval.\\n\\nBut I did **not** find an implemented promotion system like:\\n\\n```text\\nnew promotion policy\\n        ↓\\nshadow mode\\n        ↓\\nwould-have-promoted set\\n        ↓\\nreplay historical queries\\n        ↓\\npollution / stale leakage / answer regressions\\n        ↓\\nsmall canary\\n        ↓\\npromote policy to active\\n```\\n\\nThat would be valuable.\\n\\nAnd importantly, it can be added **without changing canonical FOSSIL semantics**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 13. Your “pollution filter” idea fits the architecture\\n\\nI would make it a pluggable module approximately here:\\n\\n```text\\nAgent / closeout / research / importer\\n                 |\\n                 v\\n        Candidate observation\\n                 |\\n                 v\\n       Claim proposal builder\\n                 |\\n                 v\\n      ┌─────────────────────┐\\n      │ AdmissionPolicy     │\\n      │ / PollutionFilter   │\\n      └─────────────────────┘\\n          |            |\\n       reject        admit\\n                       |\\n                  project pack\\n                       |\\n                       v\\n                 PromotionPolicy\\n                   /       \\\\\\n             not eligible   eligible\\n                              |\\n                        domain/common\\n```\\n\\nIt should never own canonical identity or rewrite evidence.\\n\\nIt should merely answer things like:\\n\\n```text\\nADMIT_AS_OBSERVATION\\nADMIT_AS_PROPOSED_CLAIM\\nREQUIRE_VERIFICATION\\nPROJECT_ONLY\\nELIGIBLE_FOR_DOMAIN_REVIEW\\nELIGIBLE_FOR_COMMON_REVIEW\\nREJECT\\n```\\n\\nThat is exactly the kind of component we can:\\n\\n1. version,\\n2. benchmark,\\n3. run in shadow,\\n4. compare against historical corpus,\\n5. canary,\\n6. replace.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 14. Versioning is already built around this style\\n\\nFOSSIL already independently versions things such as:\\n\\n- event schemas;\\n- pack schemas;\\n- ontology;\\n- graph projection;\\n- retrieval policy;\\n- models;\\n- Agent Skills;\\n- research policy;\\n- code commit;\\n- projection build manifests.\\n\\nEvents themselves can record ontology version, retrieval-policy version, model/provider/runtime, software commit, benchmark ref, etc. fileciteturn48file0L2-L2\\n\\nResearch runs likewise contain code commit, ontology version, and research-policy version. fileciteturn59file0L2-L2\\n\\nSo a future pollution policy can simply have:\\n\\n```text\\nadmission_policy:\\n    fossil-admission-v3\\n\\npromotion_policy:\\n    fossil-promotion-v2\\n```\\n\\nand every decision it makes can be reproduced.\\n\\nNo architecture rewrite needed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# So where are we actually?\\n\\nI'd characterize it this way:\\n\\n**Already built/proven:** durable history, bitemporal-style event clocks, claim/relation lifecycle, source provenance, pack boundaries, explicit promotion events, research lineage, Graphiti projection, local RAG, exact citations, abstention, temporal replay, poisoning defenses.\\n\\n**Designed but incomplete:** sophisticated context preservation/compression and polished agent-facing FOSSIL runtime.\\n\\n**Actually missing:** a rigorous **write/admission/promotion quality gate** covering closeouts, observations, benchmarks, user feedback, bugs, human verification and cross-project reuse, plus shadow/canary evaluation of that policy.\\n\\nAnd I agree with your final concern: **we should not go redesign all of FOSSIL before finishing the existing working system.**\\n\\nThe safe order is:\\n\\n```text\\nfinish durability\\n      ↓\\nfinish one complete local FOSSIL RAG runtime\\n      ↓\\nexercise it with real project work\\n      ↓\\nobserve actual pollution/promotion failures\\n      ↓\\nbuild AdmissionPolicy as a replaceable module\\n      ↓\\nshadow benchmark it\\n      ↓\\nonly then freeze a promotion policy\\n```\\n\\nThat lets the architecture **earn** the next layer from real failures instead of us inventing a giant ontology in advance.\",{},{\"_454\":8742,\"_461\":8960,\"_47\":8961,\"_465\":8962,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":8963,\"_474\":475},\"d449467d-8b76-4145-88e8-4457733e3ef1\",[8726],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":8968},1786904717.239681,{\"_1279\":1305,\"_465\":8967},{\"_1296\":1297,\"_1298\":8964,\"_1299\":8965,\"_1301\":8966,\"_1303\":1304,\"_493\":494,\"_495\":8755,\"_497\":498,\"_499\":8756,\"_501\":8756,\"_490\":60,\"_502\":60,\"_59\":60,\"_503\":504,\"_505\":14,\"_506\":8958,\"_507\":8757,\"_509\":26,\"_510\":8758,\"_512\":10},79,1786904637.8186312,1786904717.2136183,\"Worked for 1m 19s\",{},{\"_454\":8958,\"_461\":8972,\"_47\":8973,\"_465\":8974,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":8975,\"_474\":475},\"3b5056d9-0c68-45c5-9ae3-7ae0d55a9f67\",[8742],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":8987},1786904717.233472,{\"_1279\":1376,\"_1376\":8982,\"_1378\":8983},{\"_1315\":8976,\"_1296\":1317,\"_1318\":8977,\"_1320\":8978,\"_1322\":1323,\"_1299\":8965,\"_493\":494,\"_1324\":1325,\"_495\":8755,\"_497\":498,\"_499\":8756,\"_501\":8756,\"_490\":60,\"_502\":60,\"_59\":60,\"_503\":504,\"_505\":14,\"_506\":8970,\"_507\":8757,\"_509\":26,\"_510\":8758,\"_512\":10},{\"_1332\":8980},\"github\",[8979],\"/images/ecosystem/apps/github/icon.png\",[8981],\"32f3d919-2006-452f-aa6f-73b77e3a8af9\",[8984],\"528fd554-fc38-4495-9ad7-41c3d1c30109\",{\"_1381\":8985,\"_465\":1132,\"_1383\":8986,\"_1385\":26},\"Reviewed ontology structure and searched context degradation metrics\",[],{},{\"_454\":8970,\"_461\":8991,\"_47\":8992,\"_465\":8993,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":8994,\"_474\":475},\"4dfe4377-ed7a-4b32-9458-4fdaffee2d7c\",[8958],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":9007},1786904716.457662,{\"_1279\":1376,\"_1376\":8999,\"_1378\":8998},{\"_1296\":1317,\"_1320\":8995,\"_488\":8996,\"_481\":8997,\"_490\":60,\"_1397\":26,\"_1398\":26,\"_1399\":471,\"_493\":494,\"_495\":8755,\"_497\":498,\"_499\":8756,\"_501\":8756,\"_502\":60,\"_59\":60,\"_503\":504,\"_506\":8998,\"_1401\":1402,\"_1322\":1323,\"_1299\":8965,\"_1324\":1325,\"_505\":14,\"_507\":8757,\"_509\":26,\"_510\":8758,\"_512\":10},[8979],[],[],\"804bd574-eb0c-468f-bf61-dcfa2bf1921d\",[9000,9001],{\"_1381\":9004,\"_465\":9005,\"_1383\":9006,\"_1385\":26},{\"_1381\":9002,\"_465\":1132,\"_1383\":9003,\"_1385\":26},\"Assessed the evidence lifecycle\",[],\"Assessing evidence lifecycle\",\"I’m separating evidence by claim type, scope, replication, environment, freshness, and authority rather than relying on simple quality tiers. Current schemas distinguish proposals, decisions, claims, sources, and lessons, but raw artifacts can still lack lifecycle states.\",[],{},{\"_454\":8989,\"_461\":9011,\"_47\":9012,\"_465\":9013,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":9014,\"_474\":475},\"04408333-8ffc-4232-b5b5-528eac7d57a6\",[8970],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":9027},1786904706.450444,{\"_1279\":1376,\"_1376\":9019,\"_1378\":9018},{\"_1296\":1317,\"_1320\":9015,\"_488\":9016,\"_481\":9017,\"_490\":60,\"_1397\":26,\"_1398\":26,\"_1399\":471,\"_493\":494,\"_495\":8755,\"_497\":498,\"_499\":8756,\"_501\":8756,\"_502\":60,\"_59\":60,\"_503\":504,\"_506\":9018,\"_1401\":1402,\"_1322\":1323,\"_1299\":8965,\"_1324\":1325,\"_505\":14,\"_507\":8757,\"_509\":26,\"_510\":8758,\"_512\":10},[8979],[],[],\"d1bd0d24-12ba-4a33-862a-834fe1ddc284\",[9020,9021],{\"_1381\":9024,\"_465\":9025,\"_1383\":9026,\"_1385\":26},{\"_1381\":9022,\"_465\":1132,\"_1383\":9023,\"_1385\":26},\"Clarified promotion boundaries\",[],\"Clarifying promotion boundaries\",\"I’m distinguishing what exists from what’s proposed: current service interfaces support separate modules, while explicit promotion-policy abstractions aren’t implemented. Decisions and closeouts are schema-tracked, but promotion remains deliberate, evidence-backed, and manually governed rather than automatic.\",[],{},{\"_454\":9009,\"_461\":9031,\"_47\":9032,\"_49\":9033,\"_465\":9034,\"_467\":468,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":9035,\"_474\":475,\"_476\":1507},\"17c52f0c-da7d-443c-9a2e-2c6af41339e4\",[8989],{\"_1284\":1451,\"_1452\":9039,\"_472\":9040},1786904701.6498961,1786904768.461609,{\"_1279\":1280,\"_1281\":9038},{\"_1446\":9036,\"_1299\":8965,\"_493\":494,\"_499\":8756,\"_501\":8756,\"_506\":9037,\"_512\":10,\"_1448\":26},\"Searching Context Rot and Attention Degradation Metrics\",\"a330137e-4b7b-4f81-95cf-a832486981cb\",[1450],\"api_tool.call_tool\",{},{\"_454\":9029,\"_461\":9043,\"_47\":9044,\"_49\":9045,\"_465\":9046,\"_467\":468,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":9047,\"_474\":475,\"_476\":1507},[9009],{\"_1284\":1451,\"_1452\":9039,\"_472\":9050},1786904698.9497683,1786904768.481143,{\"_1279\":1280,\"_1281\":9049},{\"_1446\":9048,\"_1299\":8965,\"_493\":494,\"_499\":8756,\"_501\":8756,\"_506\":8981,\"_512\":10,\"_1448\":26},\"Reviewing Ontology Structure and Evidence Handling\",[1450],{},{\"_454\":8981,\"_461\":9054,\"_47\":9055,\"_465\":9056,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":9057,\"_474\":9039},\"8eef056d-c9af-4625-b15e-3ca7dec8a23f\",[9029],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":9060},1786904692.264913,{\"_1279\":1280,\"_1281\":9059},{\"_1444\":9058,\"_1446\":9048,\"_1299\":8965,\"_493\":494,\"_499\":8756,\"_501\":8756,\"_506\":9052,\"_512\":10,\"_1448\":26},[9048],[1450],{},{\"_454\":9052,\"_461\":9064,\"_47\":9055,\"_465\":9065,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":9066,\"_474\":475,\"_476\":1507},\"ffa50f58-7ad7-42c5-972b-e6ffd3a0d557\",[8981],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":9071},{\"_1279\":1280,\"_1281\":9069},{\"_488\":9067,\"_486\":9068,\"_490\":60,\"_1510\":26,\"_1397\":26,\"_1398\":26,\"_1422\":26,\"_1399\":471,\"_1322\":1323,\"_1299\":8965,\"_493\":494,\"_495\":8755,\"_497\":498,\"_499\":8756,\"_501\":8756,\"_502\":60,\"_59\":60,\"_503\":504,\"_505\":14,\"_506\":9062,\"_507\":8757,\"_509\":26,\"_510\":8758,\"_512\":10},[],[],[9070],\"I found the key gap: the architecture already distinguishes **source evidence, claims, lifecycle state, research decisions, model output, and closeout proposals**, but it does not yet have one unified “promotion admission controller” that scores/qualifies observations and requires shadow/canary evidence before moving project knowledge upward. That means your proposed pollution-filter idea is not architecture churn—it is a **missing enforcement module at an existing boundary**, which is much safer to prototype behind an adapter than to rewrite the core.\",{},{\"_454\":9062,\"_461\":9075,\"_47\":9076,\"_465\":9077,\"_467\":468,\"_469\":14,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":9078,\"_474\":475},\"fcab3b3d-75bc-4640-900f-9b2c47c96d25\",[9052],{\"_1284\":1285,\"_472\":9089},1786904696.051178,{\"_1279\":1376,\"_1376\":9084,\"_1378\":9085},{\"_1315\":9079,\"_1296\":1317,\"_1318\":8977,\"_1320\":9080,\"_1322\":1323,\"_1299\":8965,\"_493\":494,\"_1324\":1325,\"_495\":8755,\"_497\":498,\"_499\":8756,\"_501\":8756,\"_490\":60,\"_502\":60,\"_59\":60,\"_503\":504,\"_505\":14,\"_506\":9081,\"_507\":8757,\"_509\":26,\"_510\":8758,\"_512\":10},{\"_1332\":9082},[8979],\"cb45c3d2-4d8e-4607-8115-b7c71d5d173d\",[9083],\"bbdba12a-a45e-407e-9803-d727f2515457\",[9086],\"5f87bcf8-db13-4d8e-a4f0-0b6ea4dba259\",{\"_1381\":9087,\"_465\":1132,\"_1383\":9088,\"_1385\":26},\"Inspected research trace schemas and searched repository for promotion evidence\",[],{},{\"_454\":9073,\"_461\":9093,\"_47\":9094,\"_49\":9095,\"_465\":9096,\"_467\":468,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":9097,\"_474\":475,\"_476\":1507},\"beedd360-aefc-4921-a4e7-4a9f89c6174c\",[9062],{\"_1284\":1451,\"_1452\":9039,\"_472\":9101},1786904692.2668188,1786904768.475771,{\"_1279\":1280,\"_1281\":9100},{\"_1446\":9098,\"_1299\":8965,\"_493\":494,\"_499\":8756,\"_501\":8756,\"_506\":9099,\"_512\":10,\"_1448\":26},\"Searching Repository for Promotion Evidence Criteria\",\"f37779e1-0595-443e-92f3-fd7e1148667d\",[1450],{},{\"_454\":9091,\"_461\":9105,\"_47\":9106,\"_49\":9107,\"_465\":9108,\"_467\":468,\"_470\":471,\"_472\":9109,\"_474\":475,\"_476\":1507},\"61e9f094-9a47-4671-9722-b0e48490014f\",[9073],{\"_