losed behavior. fileciteturn15file0L3-L6\\n\\nThe number of “blockers” sounds worse than it is because this proof is intentionally very strict. There were essentially three stages: first the R2 endpoint/bucket variables were absent; then those were configured and the old code hit an R2 `HeadBucket` HTTP 400; PR #130 fixed that incompatibility by replacing the bucket-level check with a scoped `ListObjectsV2` probe. That code fix is merged and green. fileciteturn17file2L1192-L1211 fileciteturn17file4L1214-L1233 The **only blocker now** is operational: this particular ChatGPT session cannot press the equivalent of “Run workflow” to create a brand-new Actions `workflow_dispatch`. Current `main` and DKG are green; the old run simply cannot count because it targeted an older SHA. fileciteturn17file8L1280-L1299\\n\\n### How much FOSSIL work is left?\\n\\nFor the **current FOSSIL storage lane**, we are very close. I would count it as **2 substantive steps left**:\\n\\n1. **Run and pass #124 `OBJECT_STORE_LIVE`** on current `main`. This is the actual R2 writer + fresh-runner rebuild proof.\\n2. **Close out #87** using that proof plus the already-collected recovery/latency evidence. #87’s acceptance is the overall “durable S3-compatible canonical storage + disposable rebuild” milestone. fileciteturn15file0L3-L6\\n\\nUpdating #124/#94 with the resulting run IDs is bookkeeping inside those steps, not another engineering project. Also, the handoff explicitly says **do not automatically start #111** after this; its sequencing needs separate reconciliation.\\n\\n### Does this have anything to do with LiteLLM or Cortex V5?\\n\\n**No — not the task we are doing now.** `OBJECT_STORE_LIVE` is purely the **FOSSIL durable-storage layer**. It is testing FOSSIL ↔ R2/S3-compatible storage. No model calls, LiteLLM routing, Cortex execution policy, Gravebuster, or production inference are involved.\\n\\nIn fact, Cortex V5’s initial acceptance is already complete, and the 